WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.880 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.808 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.808 --> 00:00:17.080 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.880 align:middle line:84%
TOBIAS SALZ: OK,
let me start here.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.920 align:middle line:84%
So thanks for allowing me
to be back one more time.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.970 align:middle line:84%
Today I'm going to talk about
empirical models of auctions

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:33.250 align:middle line:84%
and hopefully fill in a little
bit what you have already

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.900 align:middle line:90%
learned from Glenn.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.190 align:middle line:90%
So this is the roadmap.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:44.410 align:middle line:84%
The goal is to introduce
you a little bit to how

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:48.640 align:middle line:84%
the thinking has evolved
on the empirics of auctions

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.620 align:middle line:84%
and then bring you to what is
the state of the art still,

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.420 align:middle line:84%
which is Guerre,
Perrigne, and Vuong,

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.330 align:middle line:84%
that has really had a
huge influence in the way

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:03.160 align:middle line:84%
that people analyze
auctions empirically.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.630 align:middle line:84%
The gist of all of this
is that sometimes it's

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.790 align:middle line:84%
hard to compute
equilibrium for auctions,

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.220 align:middle line:84%
but it turns out that
under the right conditions,

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:17.130 align:middle line:84%
it's actually really easy to
estimate models for auctions.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.310 align:middle line:84%
And I'll show you
how to do that.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:24.110 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to talk
about two different applications

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:25.500 align:middle line:90%
that are quite interesting.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.370 align:middle line:90%


00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.060 align:middle line:90%
So what is the relevance?

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.580 align:middle line:84%
Why do we want to
look at auctions?

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:37.310 align:middle line:84%
So for one, we are looking
at, as IO commonly says,

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:41.570 align:middle line:84%
markets where firms or
players have market power.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.100 align:middle line:84%
And a seller, let's say, in an
auction setting has, of course,

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.087 align:middle line:90%
market power.

00:01:47.087 --> 00:01:49.670 align:middle line:84%
When you think about the problem
of setting the reserve price,

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.220 align:middle line:84%
it looks almost like a
monopoly pricing problem.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.860 align:middle line:84%
So it's kind of squarely
within the topics

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.420 align:middle line:90%
that we're interested in.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.360 align:middle line:84%
There are estimates
that about 10% of GDP

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.460 align:middle line:84%
is contracted through auctions
and many different designs.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.880 align:middle line:84%
So it's important to understand
how this is being done.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.690 align:middle line:84%
Very often, the
government is involved

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.470 align:middle line:90%
as either a buyer or a seller.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:13.760 align:middle line:84%
If you think, for instance,
about spectrum auctions,

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.400 align:middle line:84%
if you think about highway
procurement projects,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:24.290 align:middle line:84%
the government gives projects
to construction firms.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.020 align:middle line:84%
Other settings, electricity
auctions, treasury auctions,

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.600 align:middle line:84%
and so on, all of
these have, of course,

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.610 align:middle line:84%
direct policy implications
because let's say

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:36.170 align:middle line:84%
you want to procure
a construction, road

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.760 align:middle line:84%
construction at the
minimal cost to taxpayers,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.360 align:middle line:84%
you want to figure out what
is the right mechanism to use.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.880 align:middle line:84%
So there is this tight
link between the way

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.890 align:middle line:84%
we analyze these
kinds of settings

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.650 align:middle line:84%
and what we can actually do as
policy makers, such as setting

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:58.528 align:middle line:84%
the reserve price, thinking
about which mechanism to use.

00:02:58.528 --> 00:03:00.590 align:middle line:84%
More from a research
point of view,

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:04.350 align:middle line:84%
auctions have been quite
fruitful in analyzing collusion.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:10.402 align:middle line:84%
So oftentimes, it's a bit easier
to get traction on collusion

00:03:10.402 --> 00:03:11.860 align:middle line:84%
than, let's say,
in repeated games.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:16.530 align:middle line:84%
And there are many nice studies,
some of which we will discuss

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:20.830 align:middle line:90%
in [14.]273.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.530 align:middle line:84%
What is also really
nice about auctions

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:29.980 align:middle line:84%
is that the rules of the
game are very defined.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.900 align:middle line:90%
We know what the strategies are.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.390 align:middle line:90%
We know who the players are.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:37.130 align:middle line:84%
And we actually see
a lot of things.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.210 align:middle line:84%
So if you think, for
instance, about something

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:45.190 align:middle line:84%
like multi-unit auctions,
like let's say in electricity,

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.880 align:middle line:84%
we actually see the whole supply
schedule that a firm bids.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.180 align:middle line:84%
That's something that
we spend a lot of time--

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.340 align:middle line:84%
supply and demand curves usually
spend a lot of time backing out,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.090 align:middle line:84%
but in some settings,
we actually directly

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:00.430 align:middle line:84%
observe at what prices
a firm wants to supply.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:04.670 align:middle line:84%
And so that gives us a
lot of empirical traction.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:08.560 align:middle line:84%
It also means that whatever
model we write down

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.730 align:middle line:84%
is oftentimes very
close to the model

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:15.310 align:middle line:90%
that firms are actually playing.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.897 align:middle line:90%
So the link here is very tight.

00:04:17.897 --> 00:04:20.480 align:middle line:84%
If you think, let's say, about
entry games or other settings--

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:22.490 align:middle line:84%
if you think about
what Amazon can do,

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.420 align:middle line:84%
Amazon can do a million
different things.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.680 align:middle line:84%
But once we narrow it down to
a specific auction setting,

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:29.690 align:middle line:84%
we kind of know what the
rules of this auction

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:33.740 align:middle line:90%
are, how people are bidding.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:37.380 align:middle line:84%
And then the participants are
oftentimes quite sophisticated.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:43.620 align:middle line:84%
So take, for instance, the
example of spectrum auctions.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:51.390 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, these are very, very
expensive bits that, let's say,

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.770 align:middle line:84%
firms like AT&T or
T-Mobile put in.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.400 align:middle line:84%
And what they're typically
doing is they hire a firm,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.700 align:middle line:90%
like Paul Milgrom's firm.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.610 align:middle line:84%
And then Paul Milgrom devises
their bidding strategy.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:05.040 align:middle line:84%
And so suffice it to say that
they're probably bidding pretty

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.960 align:middle line:90%
sophisticated in that case.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.870 align:middle line:84%
So that means that when
we write down a model

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.700 align:middle line:84%
where firms are
optimizing, maybe

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:17.760 align:middle line:84%
actually getting kind of close
to what's actually happening.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.060 align:middle line:84%
Always the goal is to
recover a distribution

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.010 align:middle line:90%
of bidder valuations.

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:26.850 align:middle line:84%
So really, to the extent
that we think of valuations,

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.890 align:middle line:84%
unobserved valuations or
unobserved, let's say,

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.687 align:middle line:84%
costs in the procurement setting
as the structural error term,

00:05:31.687 --> 00:05:33.270 align:middle line:84%
we're really interested
in backing out

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.325 align:middle line:84%
this one distribution,
the structural error term.

00:05:35.325 --> 00:05:39.580 align:middle line:90%


00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.970 align:middle line:84%
So let me just very briefly
recap single-unit auctions.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:45.730 align:middle line:84%
I know you have
seen this probably

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:49.210 align:middle line:84%
multiple times at this stage,
but I will refer back to it

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:49.970 align:middle line:90%
a few times.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:55.430 align:middle line:84%
So we have n bidders,
indexed by i.

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:58.500 align:middle line:84%
The evaluations
are denoted by Vi.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.720 align:middle line:84%
They privately observe a signal
that summarizes the information

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.160 align:middle line:90%
that they hold.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.390 align:middle line:90%
And they're bidding a bid Bi.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:09.960 align:middle line:84%
And typically the
convention is that we

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.350 align:middle line:84%
refer to random variables
as upper case letters

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.770 align:middle line:84%
and realizations as
lower case letters.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.390 align:middle line:90%


00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:22.420 align:middle line:84%
We are staying today
in the IPV paradigm.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:27.670 align:middle line:84%
That means that signals
are distributed i.i.d.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:31.220 align:middle line:84%
And we also stick to the
private value paradigm.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:34.690 align:middle line:84%
That means that I can
learn from my signal

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:38.890 align:middle line:84%
about what signals other
players have drawn.

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:42.825 align:middle line:84%
So this is a specific
case where I only

00:06:42.825 --> 00:06:45.200 align:middle line:84%
need to look at my signal,
and that's all I need to know.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.730 align:middle line:90%


00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.020 align:middle line:84%
OK, again, this is
recap, I notice.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:55.170 align:middle line:84%
But because I come
back to this, I

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.000 align:middle line:84%
want to walk you
through this once more.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.750 align:middle line:84%
So we can solve in the
first price auction

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:07.170 align:middle line:84%
case for the unique symmetric
Bayesian Nash equilibrium.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:11.220 align:middle line:84%
Bidders are maximizing
their valuation

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:15.180 align:middle line:84%
minus their bid times the
probability of winning.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:17.920 align:middle line:84%
And this is the
distribution of valuation.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:19.870 align:middle line:90%
So we want to map back to this.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:24.300 align:middle line:84%
And so we take the inverse of
the symmetric bidding function,

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.990 align:middle line:84%
mapping back from the
bit to the valuation.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:31.990 align:middle line:84%
You can take the
first order condition.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:38.280 align:middle line:84%
We get a differential equation
that you have seen before.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.580 align:middle line:84%
Later on, when I discuss GPV,
I will come back to this.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:47.370 align:middle line:84%
And here, you get with
a boundary condition

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.640 align:middle line:84%
the closed-form solution
for the bidding function.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.370 align:middle line:90%


00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:56.730 align:middle line:84%
So let me just
comment, first of all,

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:59.400 align:middle line:84%
that this doesn't have a
great economic interpretation,

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:05.010 align:middle line:84%
but it's equal to the expected
value of the valuation

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:08.520 align:middle line:84%
of the other remaining bidders,
given that my valuation

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.500 align:middle line:90%
is higher than theirs.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.150 align:middle line:90%
So that's what this is equal to.

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:15.840 align:middle line:84%
So that's a bit
more interpretable.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.510 align:middle line:84%
The other thing from an
empirical point of view,

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:21.330 align:middle line:84%
if you stare at this
expression here,

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:25.260 align:middle line:90%
this is an integral over a CDF.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:30.173 align:middle line:84%
And for most common
distribution functions,

00:08:30.173 --> 00:08:31.590 align:middle line:84%
and especially if
we do not impose

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:35.070 align:middle line:84%
any parametric
restrictions, that does not

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.250 align:middle line:90%
have a closed form solution.

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:37.770 align:middle line:84%
That means that from an
empirical point of view,

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.570 align:middle line:84%
this is kind of a
messy thing here

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:42.940 align:middle line:84%
because we have to
numerically integrate this.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:47.430 align:middle line:84%
And so I come back why
that's not a great thing

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:49.805 align:middle line:84%
and how to possibly
sidestep this.

00:08:49.805 --> 00:08:55.490 align:middle line:90%


00:08:55.490 --> 00:09:01.970 align:middle line:84%
So here is the solution
with a reserve price.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:05.090 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let me come
to the first paper

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:09.710 align:middle line:84%
that I wanted to discuss today,
Laffont, Ossard, and Vuong.

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:14.070 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the very early
empirical auction papers.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:16.230 align:middle line:90%
It has some really nice ideas.

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:21.830 align:middle line:84%
It's not really the way
the literature has gone,

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:25.310 align:middle line:84%
but I think it helps
the way to understand

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:27.710 align:middle line:90%
how the thinking has evolved.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:34.250 align:middle line:84%
So again, we're in the
symmetric IPV setting.

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:39.207 align:middle line:84%
These are descending auctions
with a reserve price.

00:09:39.207 --> 00:09:40.790 align:middle line:84%
These kinds of
auctions are oftentimes

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:44.090 align:middle line:84%
used for perishables, things
that have to be sold quickly.

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:46.940 align:middle line:84%
Because as soon as
somebody stops--

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.250 align:middle line:84%
you only need one bit
to stop the auction.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:53.480 align:middle line:84%
The setting here is a little
town in the south of France

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:56.900 align:middle line:84%
where they're selling baskets of
eggplants in a wholesale market.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:58.790 align:middle line:84%
So there are sellers
that buy these baskets

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:03.420 align:middle line:84%
and then go to a retail
market and sell those.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:08.940 align:middle line:84%
The imposing log normal
distribution on the valuations.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.990 align:middle line:84%
And the goal is to estimate
the parameters of this log

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.570 align:middle line:90%
normal distribution.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.270 align:middle line:84%
And they have data
from 81 auctions

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:21.935 align:middle line:84%
with the same set of bidders
in each of the auctions.

00:10:21.935 --> 00:10:25.300 align:middle line:90%


00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:32.080 align:middle line:84%
OK, so how can we make progress
on estimating this distribution

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:33.926 align:middle line:90%
of valuations?

00:10:33.926 --> 00:10:37.190 align:middle line:90%


00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:39.950 align:middle line:90%
So one idea is to use the--

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:43.040 align:middle line:84%
we have just seen a solution for
first-price, sealed bid auction.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:47.180 align:middle line:84%
One idea is to use the strategic
equivalence between sealed bid,

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:48.920 align:middle line:90%
first-price auctions--

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:51.950 align:middle line:84%
with first-price, sealed
bid auction and use

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.220 align:middle line:90%
maximum likelihood.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:55.830 align:middle line:90%


00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:59.300 align:middle line:84%
So let's see how
far this gets us.

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:03.860 align:middle line:84%
So I want to maximize some
likelihood function, where

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:06.380 align:middle line:84%
f is given by this log normal
as the density of the log

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:08.540 align:middle line:84%
normal distribution
and the vector of bids

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.640 align:middle line:90%
that are observed.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:16.160 align:middle line:84%
And I know here, with a
reserve price built in,

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:19.130 align:middle line:84%
I know that this is my
closed-form solution

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:20.850 align:middle line:90%
to the bidding function.

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.730 align:middle line:84%
So now, if you think about
how to actually do this,

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.140 align:middle line:90%
how would you do this?

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:32.250 align:middle line:84%
For a given parameter guess,
I know what these objects are.

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:33.635 align:middle line:90%
What I observe are the bids.

00:11:33.635 --> 00:11:37.270 align:middle line:90%


00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:40.660 align:middle line:84%
But I want to evaluate
the likelihood, of course,

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:44.710 align:middle line:84%
at the valuations,
which I do not observe.

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:49.390 align:middle line:84%
So what I need to do is for
a given guess of parameters,

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:53.080 align:middle line:90%
I need to compute this thing.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:58.390 align:middle line:84%
And I need to find the v
under this expression that

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:02.520 align:middle line:84%
makes this equal to
the bid that I observe.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:07.890 align:middle line:84%
So I basically need to conduct a
line search, where I repeatedly

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:14.340 align:middle line:84%
guess v's until this
expression is equal to my b.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:18.510 align:middle line:84%
And while I'm doing this, I
need to repeatedly evaluate

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:20.498 align:middle line:84%
this thing that
I told you is not

00:12:20.498 --> 00:12:22.290 align:middle line:84%
super straightforward
to compute because it

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:25.530 align:middle line:90%
has this integral over a CDF.

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:27.030 align:middle line:84%
So you need to
numerically integrate

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:31.150 align:middle line:84%
this to support that
integration limits are changing.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:33.005 align:middle line:84%
That makes all of
this not so easy.

00:12:33.005 --> 00:12:36.670 align:middle line:90%


00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:43.020 align:middle line:84%
So you probably get the sense
that this doesn't work great.

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.680 align:middle line:84%
In the early literature by
papers by Harry Parr and others,

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:48.450 align:middle line:90%
they have done this.

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:54.210 align:middle line:84%
But this is no longer really
the way people estimate auctions

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:54.970 align:middle line:90%
these days.

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:58.980 align:middle line:84%
It's computationally costly,
as I hope I made clear here.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.140 align:middle line:84%
Another issue is
that we actually only

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:02.880 align:middle line:90%
observed the winning bid here.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:04.560 align:middle line:84%
So what we would
actually have to do

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:08.040 align:middle line:84%
is we have to first get the
expression for the likelihood

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:11.370 align:middle line:84%
of the winning bid, which is
something we can, of course, do.

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:16.080 align:middle line:84%
But there's an extra,
more fundamental problem

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:24.510 align:middle line:84%
that the support of the
distribution of the winning bid

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:28.960 align:middle line:84%
depends on the parameters,
and in particular

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:35.350 align:middle line:84%
on the max of the
largest out of N minus 1

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:38.800 align:middle line:84%
draws on the distribution of
valuations and the reserve

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:39.940 align:middle line:90%
price.

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:42.910 align:middle line:84%
And that violates the
regularity conditions

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:44.030 align:middle line:90%
of maximum likelihood.

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:45.447 align:middle line:84%
So maximum likelihood
in this case

00:13:45.447 --> 00:13:48.100 align:middle line:90%
is not a consistent estimator.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:50.830 align:middle line:84%
So there again,
there's early work

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:54.680 align:middle line:84%
by Harry Paarsch that tries
to find ways around this.

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:58.660 align:middle line:84%
But as you will
see, there are much

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:00.360 align:middle line:90%
better ways to go about this.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:04.030 align:middle line:90%


00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:07.030 align:middle line:90%
OK, so here's another idea.

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:14.080 align:middle line:84%
So instead of having to map back
from the expression for my bid

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:17.290 align:middle line:84%
to my valuation and then
evaluate the likelihood, what

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:20.710 align:middle line:84%
I could instead do is I
could simulate the object

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:22.180 align:middle line:90%
that I observe.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:28.390 align:middle line:84%
So I see a bid, and with
the closed form expression

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:33.080 align:middle line:84%
for my bidding function, I
can simulate the integral

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:35.530 align:middle line:84%
and match this integral,
the expected winning bid,

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:38.130 align:middle line:90%
to the observed winning bid.

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:42.490 align:middle line:84%
OK, so here is the expression
for the expected winning

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:49.590 align:middle line:84%
bid, which integrates out
over this bidding function

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:55.200 align:middle line:84%
and here uses the expression
for the density of the highest

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:56.900 align:middle line:90%
valuation.

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:59.700 align:middle line:84%
And so when you
plug this in, you

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:04.980 align:middle line:84%
can simplify this a little
bit, and you get an expression

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:07.500 align:middle line:90%
that looks like this here.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:10.330 align:middle line:84%
And typically,
complicated integrals,

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:13.020 align:middle line:84%
one question you should always
ask yourself is, instead

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:16.030 align:middle line:84%
of computing this
integral exactly,

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:17.590 align:middle line:84%
do I somehow want
to simulate this?

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:19.890 align:middle line:84%
Because simulation is
often oftentimes easier.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:22.620 align:middle line:84%
In BLP, that's something
that we typically

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.630 align:middle line:90%
do for the random coefficient.

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:28.560 align:middle line:84%
We take a bunch of random
draws and then integrate out.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.200 align:middle line:84%
So that's something
one could do.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:34.720 align:middle line:90%
It's still somewhat cumbersome.

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:37.240 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you have to take random
draws from the log normal.

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:39.030 align:middle line:90%
That's not easy with--

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:43.870 align:middle line:84%
that's actually very easy with
modern programming languages.

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:47.400 align:middle line:84%
But then you still have to-- for
every draw of the evaluations,

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:51.540 align:middle line:84%
I have to compute
this expression here

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:53.530 align:middle line:90%
over which I take an average.

00:15:53.530 --> 00:15:57.090 align:middle line:84%
And here still I have to compute
this integral over a CDF.

00:15:57.090 --> 00:16:00.750 align:middle line:84%
So it's already much better
than what we had before,

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:02.890 align:middle line:84%
but it's still
somewhat cumbersome.

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:06.930 align:middle line:90%


00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:08.075 align:middle line:90%
Any questions so far?

00:16:08.075 --> 00:16:14.620 align:middle line:90%


00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:23.140 align:middle line:84%
So here's a clever insight that
Laffont, Ossard, and Vuong had.

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:26.530 align:middle line:84%
We stick with simulation,
but we can use the revenue

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:28.680 align:middle line:90%
equivalence theorem.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:32.290 align:middle line:84%
So I've seen that Glenn
has talked about this.

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:34.470 align:middle line:90%
So I'm just repeating this here.

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:39.540 align:middle line:84%
So we're assuming a
risk-neutral IPV setting

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:42.600 align:middle line:90%
with atomless signal support.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:45.690 align:middle line:84%
We know that in this
case, any mechanism which

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:48.900 align:middle line:84%
is efficient in that it awards
the object to the bidder

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:51.360 align:middle line:84%
with the highest signal
or with the lowest cost

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:56.940 align:middle line:84%
and leaves the weakest
bidder with zero surplus,

00:16:56.940 --> 00:17:00.510 align:middle line:90%
it's the same expected revenue.

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:02.535 align:middle line:90%
And so how can we use this?

00:17:02.535 --> 00:17:06.130 align:middle line:90%


00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:15.010 align:middle line:84%
We know that using this
result, for this specific sets

00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:20.319 align:middle line:84%
of assumptions, the
expected bid that we

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:21.819 align:middle line:84%
get from this
closed-form solution

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:26.109 align:middle line:84%
for the first-price
sealed bid auction,

00:17:26.109 --> 00:17:31.510 align:middle line:84%
here using again the
expectation over the valuations

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:37.120 align:middle line:84%
of the strongest bidder, so
the highest order statistic

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:39.370 align:middle line:84%
and taking into account
the reserve price,

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:42.980 align:middle line:84%
that the expected
revenue from those bids

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:46.580 align:middle line:84%
has to be equal to
the expected revenue

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:50.900 align:middle line:84%
from a second-price auction
within the same setting.

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:54.980 align:middle line:84%
And remember, in a
second-price auction,

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:56.810 align:middle line:84%
bidders' weakly
dominant strategy

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:59.560 align:middle line:90%
is to bid their valuations.

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:02.740 align:middle line:84%
And so here we can basically
make use of the fact

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:07.030 align:middle line:84%
that I no longer have to
compute any complicated bidding

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:07.850 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:11.516 align:middle line:84%
I can directly take an
expectation over valuations.

00:18:11.516 --> 00:18:19.410 align:middle line:90%


00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:23.570 align:middle line:90%
So how would one then do this?

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:26.450 align:middle line:90%
This is actually really simple.

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:28.460 align:middle line:84%
So you generate-- you
have your log normal.

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:32.510 align:middle line:84%
You generate some-- for
a given simulation draw,

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:35.420 align:middle line:84%
you generate a vector
of evaluations.

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:39.980 align:middle line:84%
You take the second highest
one and set the winning bid

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:43.820 align:middle line:84%
as the maximum of that
realization and the reserve

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:45.210 align:middle line:90%
price.

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:48.870 align:middle line:84%
You do this a bunch of times,
and you get an expectation

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:50.670 align:middle line:90%
for the winning bid.

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:53.010 align:middle line:84%
You do this for
every parameter guess

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:55.660 align:middle line:84%
and you minimize the
squared deviation.

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:57.510 align:middle line:84%
So this is nonlinear
least squares

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:02.320 align:middle line:90%
from the observed winning bids.

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:08.380 align:middle line:84%
So this is to show that
sometimes you can use the theory

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:10.100 align:middle line:90%
to make your life really easy.

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:11.920 align:middle line:84%
In this case, this
clever insight

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:15.940 align:middle line:84%
that instead of estimating this
with a first-price auction,

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:18.940 align:middle line:84%
we just use a second-price
auction instead.

00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:20.730 align:middle line:84%
And this makes our
life much, much easier.

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:27.180 align:middle line:90%


00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:31.150 align:middle line:84%
So let me comment on
this a little bit.

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:34.620 align:middle line:84%
So this is, as neat as this,
actually not an approach

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:39.690 align:middle line:84%
that's widely used
anymore in empirical work.

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:42.330 align:middle line:84%
So one reason is
certainly that there

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:48.090 align:middle line:84%
are many different ways that
revenue equivalence breaks--

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:53.240 align:middle line:84%
asymmetric bidders, risk
aversion, endogenous--

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:59.040 align:middle line:84%
I mean, cost of entering an
auction, you know, collusion--

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:05.030 align:middle line:84%
So many things that we believe
are important in the real world

00:20:05.030 --> 00:20:07.040 align:middle line:90%
lead this result to break.

00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:11.480 align:middle line:84%
And so it might
be maybe assuming

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:16.610 align:middle line:84%
a little too much to
rely on this result.

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:21.680 align:middle line:84%
The other reason is that people,
at least in this literature,

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:26.180 align:middle line:84%
in the auction literature, have
moved away from strong reliance

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:27.270 align:middle line:90%
on functional forms.

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:30.920 align:middle line:84%
So in this paper,
we just assumed

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:34.850 align:middle line:84%
a log normal distribution,
and people are typically not

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:36.980 align:middle line:84%
comfortable to say
from the get go,

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:40.940 align:middle line:84%
oh, this is what the
distribution of valuations

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:44.450 align:middle line:84%
looks like, or this is
the parametric family

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:47.900 align:middle line:90%
which in I believe it lies.

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:51.800 align:middle line:84%
And so instead, what
people are doing is

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:55.370 align:middle line:84%
they typically try
to identify models,

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:57.350 align:middle line:84%
what is called
nonparametrically,

00:20:57.350 --> 00:21:02.060 align:middle line:84%
and place strong emphasis on
formal identification results.

00:21:02.060 --> 00:21:06.020 align:middle line:84%
So I want to now just have
a digression of one slide

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:10.640 align:middle line:84%
to talk you through a little
bit what that actually means.

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:11.420 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:16.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so was it the
case that for each option,

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:20.190 align:middle line:84%
we're assuming that the bidders
have a new valuation drawn?

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:22.128 align:middle line:84%
TOBIAS SALZ: Yes,
in this setting.

00:21:22.128 --> 00:21:22.670 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:21:22.670 --> 00:21:24.440 align:middle line:84%
And so I think at
the very beginning,

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:27.630 align:middle line:84%
you said that there's the
same 11 bidders throughout.

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:30.203 align:middle line:84%
And so for a given
bidder, the valuation

00:21:30.203 --> 00:21:31.370 align:middle line:90%
draws are correlated at all?

00:21:31.370 --> 00:21:33.335 align:middle line:84%
Or are we also assuming
that those are independent?

00:21:33.335 --> 00:21:35.270 align:middle line:84%
TOBIAS SALZ: In this
particular application,

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:37.103 align:middle line:84%
they're assuming that
those are independent.

00:21:37.103 --> 00:21:38.930 align:middle line:84%
But that's a good
question, that you

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:40.790 align:middle line:84%
may think that
some bidders maybe

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:43.680 align:middle line:84%
have better resale
opportunities,

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:44.930 align:middle line:90%
have a better retail location.

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:51.080 align:middle line:84%
And for them, their valuations
are kind of systematically

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:53.240 align:middle line:84%
different over a
longer time horizon.

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:56.390 align:middle line:84%
And these kinds of
asymmetries are oftentimes,

00:21:56.390 --> 00:21:59.900 align:middle line:84%
in many modern applications
of auction models,

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:03.380 align:middle line:84%
people try to take
them into account.

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:10.590 align:middle line:84%
They make life very complicated
because for asymmetric auctions,

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:13.740 align:middle line:84%
there's typically no closed-form
solution for the bidding

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:16.500 align:middle line:84%
function in the
first-price auction case.

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:24.430 align:middle line:84%
So it's estimation, as I will
show you in two slides, is easy.

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:28.180 align:middle line:84%
But equilibrium computation
is hard with asymmetries.

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:32.060 align:middle line:90%


00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:34.280 align:middle line:84%
So just very
briefly, what does it

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:37.730 align:middle line:84%
mean for a model
to be identified?

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:39.770 align:middle line:84%
People use the
term identification

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:40.830 align:middle line:90%
in many different ways.

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:46.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm telling you how this term is
used in the auction literature.

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:50.870 align:middle line:84%
So what people have
in mind here is that--

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:53.810 align:middle line:84%
so first, we are
settling on the game,

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:56.030 align:middle line:84%
and we're settling
typically on a solution

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:59.270 align:middle line:84%
concept for this game,
let's say, a Bayesian Nash

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:00.380 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:06.080 align:middle line:84%
And given that, a
model is identified

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:09.860 align:middle line:84%
if the joint distribution of
bidder utilities and signals

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:13.370 align:middle line:84%
is uniquely determined by the
joint distribution of things

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:15.002 align:middle line:90%
that I observe.

00:23:15.002 --> 00:23:16.460 align:middle line:84%
Things that I
observe are typically

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:22.530 align:middle line:84%
bids or bids and entry behavior,
or in some settings, bids

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:26.840 align:middle line:84%
and, let's say, whether
I'm drilling this oil field

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:30.740 align:middle line:84%
after the auction or
not, so things that are

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:32.440 align:middle line:90%
observable to the researcher.

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:36.120 align:middle line:90%


00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:39.480 align:middle line:84%
OK, so here's a slightly more
formal way of saying this.

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:43.410 align:middle line:84%
Let m star be the true vector
of functions and distributions,

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:47.100 align:middle line:84%
and let P of m denote
the joint distribution

00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:49.050 align:middle line:84%
of observable variables
under the assumption

00:23:49.050 --> 00:23:51.340 align:middle line:84%
that the data is
generated under m.

00:23:51.340 --> 00:23:54.715 align:middle line:84%
So think of P as basically
a mapping from my model.

00:23:54.715 --> 00:23:56.340 align:middle line:84%
And just to make
things simple for now,

00:23:56.340 --> 00:24:01.920 align:middle line:84%
think about your model is
the distribution that you

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:04.930 align:middle line:90%
assume for bid as valuations.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:09.780 align:middle line:84%
And P basically maps that with
some assumption on which game

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:15.820 align:middle line:84%
is being played and what
equilibrium to the data.

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:20.900 align:middle line:84%
And we say that m star is
identified in a class of models

00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:29.920 align:middle line:84%
if and only if for all models in
this set, whenever my model is

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:31.390 align:middle line:84%
different from m
star, I would also

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:32.690 align:middle line:90%
observe something different.

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:35.550 align:middle line:90%


00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:38.850 align:middle line:84%
And so what is important
here is that this--

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:42.460 align:middle line:90%
we typically do this--

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:44.370 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we do this
under the assumption

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:47.790 align:middle line:84%
that we have infinite
amount of data.

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:51.840 align:middle line:84%
So this is not something where
we're worried about small sample

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:52.510 align:middle line:90%
properties.

00:24:52.510 --> 00:24:55.320 align:middle line:84%
This is really in
the ideal case.

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:56.910 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes it might
be rare events

00:24:56.910 --> 00:24:58.860 align:middle line:90%
that tell two models apart.

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:01.710 align:middle line:84%
Here we're assuming we have
these rare events that allow us

00:25:01.710 --> 00:25:05.270 align:middle line:90%
to tell the two models apart.

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:08.450 align:middle line:84%
So now with this, I can tell
you what it means for something

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:10.160 align:middle line:90%
to be nonparametric.

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:15.800 align:middle line:84%
So first, a parametric
model is if I restrict m

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:18.360 align:middle line:84%
to be a subset of a
finite dimensional space.

00:25:18.360 --> 00:25:23.960 align:middle line:84%
Let's say I'm searching over
the set of normal distributions

00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:27.140 align:middle line:90%
or exponential distributions.

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:30.080 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes one parameter
may be enough.

00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:32.900 align:middle line:84%
Or I could even
allow for, let's say,

00:25:32.900 --> 00:25:35.750 align:middle line:84%
a search over different kinds
of parametric distributions

00:25:35.750 --> 00:25:42.530 align:middle line:84%
that would still be
parametric or semiparametric.

00:25:42.530 --> 00:25:48.050 align:middle line:84%
If m, however, is not a subset
of a finite dimensional space,

00:25:48.050 --> 00:25:52.100 align:middle line:84%
we say that it's semiparametric
if a subset of functions

00:25:52.100 --> 00:25:54.650 align:middle line:84%
lies in the finite dimensional
space and nonparametric

00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:57.300 align:middle line:84%
if none of them lies in a
finite dimensional space.

00:25:57.300 --> 00:26:00.110 align:middle line:84%
So thing about
nonparametric basically

00:26:00.110 --> 00:26:05.020 align:middle line:84%
means that you can have any sort
of weird density of valuations.

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:07.800 align:middle line:90%
It could have different modes.

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:09.695 align:middle line:84%
You're not placing any
restrictions on it.

00:26:09.695 --> 00:26:16.020 align:middle line:90%


00:26:16.020 --> 00:26:17.560 align:middle line:90%
So one more word on this.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:23.570 align:middle line:84%
So how do we actually go about
thinking about identification?

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:25.800 align:middle line:84%
So the typical
approach is what is

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:28.950 align:middle line:84%
called the constructive
approach, is to try and express

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:32.780 align:middle line:84%
the things that I do not know in
terms of things that I observe.

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:35.530 align:middle line:90%


00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:41.890 align:middle line:84%
Because then it's
crystal clear that what

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:46.660 align:middle line:84%
I'm looking for-- the
primitive that I'm looking for

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:50.710 align:middle line:84%
can be expressed in
terms of data directly.

00:26:50.710 --> 00:26:53.592 align:middle line:84%
So just very, very
simple example for this.

00:26:53.592 --> 00:26:55.050 align:middle line:84%
Think about a
second-price auction.

00:26:55.050 --> 00:27:01.500 align:middle line:90%


00:27:01.500 --> 00:27:04.830 align:middle line:84%
So again, for us,
we're typically

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:09.770 align:middle line:84%
interested in learning the
distribution of valuations.

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:13.085 align:middle line:84%
And let's say we assume
that the bidders here

00:27:13.085 --> 00:27:15.490 align:middle line:84%
are playing the weakly
dominant strategy of bidding

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:17.036 align:middle line:90%
their own valuation.

00:27:17.036 --> 00:27:19.870 align:middle line:90%


00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:22.220 align:middle line:84%
I hope that-- you
see this, right?

00:27:22.220 --> 00:27:24.670 align:middle line:84%
This is basically
almost immediate.

00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:28.120 align:middle line:84%
I know that, in
this case, my bids

00:27:28.120 --> 00:27:31.180 align:middle line:90%
are equal to the valuations.

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:33.680 align:middle line:84%
This means I directly
observe the valuations.

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:37.870 align:middle line:84%
So it's trivially identified
because I can express--

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:40.840 align:middle line:84%
every bid as valuation, I
have a direct correspondence

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:42.255 align:middle line:90%
to that bidder's bid.

00:27:42.255 --> 00:27:45.520 align:middle line:90%


00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:49.020 align:middle line:84%
So what, however, if only
transaction prices are observed?

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:51.430 align:middle line:90%
OK, so now I can do this.

00:27:51.430 --> 00:27:54.970 align:middle line:84%
I can't directly express
the unknown thing, my v

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:56.310 align:middle line:90%
in terms of the known thing b.

00:27:56.310 --> 00:27:59.990 align:middle line:90%


00:27:59.990 --> 00:28:02.910 align:middle line:84%
So here the following
result comes in handy.

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:07.510 align:middle line:84%
So first, as you know,
the winning bid--

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:13.080 align:middle line:84%
sorry, the transaction price
is given by the second highest

00:28:13.080 --> 00:28:13.786 align:middle line:90%
valuation.

00:28:13.786 --> 00:28:16.930 align:middle line:90%


00:28:16.930 --> 00:28:22.610 align:middle line:84%
So what I do observe is the
second highest order statistic.

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:24.570 align:middle line:90%
And what we typically--

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:27.080 align:middle line:84%
not always, but what we
typically assume in auctions

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:30.200 align:middle line:84%
is that what we also know
is the number of bidders.

00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:33.410 align:middle line:84%
This is empirically not
always crystal clear

00:28:33.410 --> 00:28:35.060 align:middle line:84%
that that's really
something that's

00:28:35.060 --> 00:28:38.760 align:middle line:84%
observed without measurement
error or precisely observed.

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:42.010 align:middle line:84%
But that's typically
an assumption.

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:44.450 align:middle line:90%
So I know the number of bidders.

00:28:44.450 --> 00:28:48.890 align:middle line:84%
I also know the second
highest order statistic.

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:51.320 align:middle line:90%
I do not know f of v directly.

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:53.840 align:middle line:84%
I only know the second
highest order statistic.

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:58.730 align:middle line:90%
So can I make progress here?

00:28:58.730 --> 00:29:01.070 align:middle line:84%
The answer is yes
because of this result.

00:29:01.070 --> 00:29:03.650 align:middle line:84%
I think Glenn in
his lecture had also

00:29:03.650 --> 00:29:05.130 align:middle line:84%
an expression for
order statistics.

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:09.290 align:middle line:84%
This is yet a different
expression for order statistics.

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:13.760 align:middle line:84%
It makes it pretty clear why,
from any order statistic,

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:17.750 align:middle line:84%
we can identify the
underlying distribution.

00:29:17.750 --> 00:29:23.120 align:middle line:84%
And that's because if you
stare at this expression here,

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:24.890 align:middle line:84%
everything on the
right-hand side

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:30.350 align:middle line:84%
here is known except for f of
v. I know N. I know which order

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:32.270 align:middle line:90%
statistic I observe.

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:36.410 align:middle line:84%
And this is an integral that's
strictly increasing in f of v.

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:41.090 align:middle line:84%
So that means I can
pointwise invert this thing.

00:29:41.090 --> 00:29:45.080 align:middle line:90%
I know this here pointwise.

00:29:45.080 --> 00:29:50.240 align:middle line:84%
And so for every
point in the support,

00:29:50.240 --> 00:30:00.320 align:middle line:84%
I can basically map this to
an f of an underlying f of v.

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:04.250 align:middle line:84%
So again, what this shows is
that even though we do not

00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:11.155 align:middle line:84%
observe all the bids, we
can still make progress.

00:30:11.155 --> 00:30:12.780 align:middle line:84%
And so what this, of
course, also means

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:16.560 align:middle line:84%
is that any order statistic
identifies any other order

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:17.470 align:middle line:90%
statistic.

00:30:17.470 --> 00:30:23.640 align:middle line:90%


00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:26.700 align:middle line:84%
So I hope this gives you some
sense of how people normally

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:27.740 align:middle line:90%
approach this.

00:30:27.740 --> 00:30:30.840 align:middle line:90%
So again, here the idea was to--

00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:35.483 align:middle line:84%
just coming back to this
once more-- this is theta,

00:30:35.483 --> 00:30:36.900 align:middle line:84%
this is the thing
that I'm looking

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.540 align:middle line:84%
for, this is a known
function that I can invert.

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:44.710 align:middle line:84%
So I can express f of
v in terms of theta

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:46.080 align:middle line:90%
or known functions of theta.

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:52.530 align:middle line:90%


00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:56.730 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is really
the landmark paper

00:30:56.730 --> 00:30:59.985 align:middle line:84%
in this literature, Guerre,
Perrigne, and Vuong.

00:30:59.985 --> 00:31:02.490 align:middle line:90%


00:31:02.490 --> 00:31:08.310 align:middle line:84%
Think of this as a paper for
auctions as BLP is for demand.

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:13.110 align:middle line:84%
This has really created
a big literature.

00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:17.333 align:middle line:84%
The idea of the paper is, again,
similar to these easy steps

00:31:17.333 --> 00:31:18.750 align:middle line:84%
that I've just
walked you through,

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:21.780 align:middle line:84%
is that we can directly
express the unobserved

00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:26.910 align:middle line:84%
valuations in terms of
things that we observe.

00:31:26.910 --> 00:31:30.210 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm not going through
all the details here,

00:31:30.210 --> 00:31:33.750 align:middle line:84%
but this also works
if we do only observe

00:31:33.750 --> 00:31:35.940 align:middle line:90%
the winning bid in most cases.

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:40.080 align:middle line:84%
So I promised you I'd come back
once more to this first order

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:45.820 align:middle line:84%
condition here from
our first-price auction

00:31:45.820 --> 00:31:46.445 align:middle line:90%
sealed bidding.

00:31:46.445 --> 00:31:50.160 align:middle line:90%


00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:54.510 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about this,
it looks pretty daunting,

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:56.150 align:middle line:90%
What we observe are the bids.

00:31:56.150 --> 00:31:59.230 align:middle line:90%


00:31:59.230 --> 00:32:04.900 align:middle line:84%
And we know the number of
bidders, but we do not know v,

00:32:04.900 --> 00:32:08.140 align:middle line:84%
we do not know the
distribution of valuations,

00:32:08.140 --> 00:32:11.650 align:middle line:84%
and we do not know the
derivative of the bidding

00:32:11.650 --> 00:32:13.810 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:32:13.810 --> 00:32:19.630 align:middle line:84%
You still have the
insight that GPV had,

00:32:19.630 --> 00:32:22.090 align:middle line:84%
which is that because of the
monotonicity of the bidding

00:32:22.090 --> 00:32:27.910 align:middle line:84%
function, a percentile
of the bid distribution

00:32:27.910 --> 00:32:30.850 align:middle line:84%
has to correspond to a
percentile of the distribution

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:32.710 align:middle line:90%
of valuations.

00:32:32.710 --> 00:32:35.320 align:middle line:84%
OK, so the 20th percentile
of bids that are observed

00:32:35.320 --> 00:32:37.300 align:middle line:90%
in my data must--

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:40.090 align:middle line:84%
that percentile must have been
submitted by somebody who is

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:43.726 align:middle line:84%
in the 20th percentile of the
distribution of valuations.

00:32:43.726 --> 00:32:46.750 align:middle line:90%


00:32:46.750 --> 00:32:51.670 align:middle line:84%
So that means that we know
that G of b is equal to f of v.

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:54.790 align:middle line:84%
And we also know that G of b is
actually given by this object

00:32:54.790 --> 00:33:01.460 align:middle line:84%
here, G of the symmetric
bidding function of v.

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:04.010 align:middle line:84%
And then you can take the
derivative on both sides,

00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:09.710 align:middle line:84%
and you get that G of b
is equal to f of v times

00:33:09.710 --> 00:33:12.055 align:middle line:84%
1 over the derivative
of the bidding function.

00:33:12.055 --> 00:33:15.010 align:middle line:90%


00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:20.650 align:middle line:84%
So now you probably see that
we can make some progress here.

00:33:20.650 --> 00:33:28.930 align:middle line:84%
I can basically replace f of v
here by G of b times beta prime.

00:33:28.930 --> 00:33:34.490 align:middle line:84%
Beta prime cancels out, and I
can replace f of v by G of b.

00:33:34.490 --> 00:33:38.040 align:middle line:90%


00:33:38.040 --> 00:33:43.710 align:middle line:84%
And that means that I
can-- again, as I said,

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:46.040 align:middle line:84%
this is what is called
a constructive approach.

00:33:46.040 --> 00:33:48.660 align:middle line:84%
We can express
valuations in terms

00:33:48.660 --> 00:33:54.960 align:middle line:84%
of bids and distributions,
CDFs and PDFs of bids

00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:55.890 align:middle line:90%
that we observe.

00:33:55.890 --> 00:33:59.460 align:middle line:84%
So all of this here on the
right-hand side is theta.

00:33:59.460 --> 00:34:01.360 align:middle line:84%
This is the thing
that we do not know.

00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:04.290 align:middle line:84%
So for each bidder
separately, we

00:34:04.290 --> 00:34:07.200 align:middle line:84%
can figure out what is
that bidder's valuation.

00:34:07.200 --> 00:34:10.781 align:middle line:84%
And so this should also
make it clear why this is--

00:34:10.781 --> 00:34:12.989 align:middle line:84%
automatically, it implies
that it's nonparametrically

00:34:12.989 --> 00:34:18.880 align:middle line:84%
identified because we don't
place any restrictions.

00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:22.989 align:middle line:84%
For every separate
bidder, the bid

00:34:22.989 --> 00:34:27.310 align:middle line:84%
is exactly rationalized
by a specific valuation

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:29.054 align:middle line:84%
that I can back
out point by point.

00:34:29.054 --> 00:34:35.449 align:middle line:90%


00:34:35.449 --> 00:34:39.300 align:middle line:84%
So this is how this is
derived in the paper.

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:40.500 align:middle line:90%
There's an even easier way.

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:41.833 align:middle line:90%
I want to show you this as well.

00:34:41.833 --> 00:34:46.610 align:middle line:84%
And once you think about it
this way, it's sort of ex-post

00:34:46.610 --> 00:34:49.020 align:middle line:90%
obvious that you can do this.

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:51.800 align:middle line:84%
But oftentimes with
good papers, they're

00:34:51.800 --> 00:34:54.290 align:middle line:90%
ex-post obvious at least.

00:34:54.290 --> 00:34:57.139 align:middle line:84%
So there's another
way of deriving this.

00:34:57.139 --> 00:35:00.890 align:middle line:84%
This, by the way, a trick
that's used a lot in IO.

00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:05.820 align:middle line:84%
So that's something that
generally you should remember.

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:09.800 align:middle line:84%
So we observed the bids
in the data, right?

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:13.040 align:middle line:84%
And we are already
committed to our assumption

00:35:13.040 --> 00:35:15.890 align:middle line:84%
that this is coming
from some equilibrium,

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:18.410 align:middle line:84%
So that means that we observed
the equilibrium distribution

00:35:18.410 --> 00:35:19.420 align:middle line:90%
of bids in the data.

00:35:19.420 --> 00:35:23.640 align:middle line:90%


00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:29.950 align:middle line:84%
And at the point where
we're willing to stick

00:35:29.950 --> 00:35:35.020 align:middle line:84%
to this assumption, we can
write down a bid as a problem

00:35:35.020 --> 00:35:37.030 align:middle line:84%
instead as what
essentially looks

00:35:37.030 --> 00:35:41.230 align:middle line:84%
like a single-agent
problem, somebody

00:35:41.230 --> 00:35:44.680 align:middle line:84%
who's maximizing against
this distribution of bids

00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:46.125 align:middle line:90%
that's just given by data.

00:35:46.125 --> 00:35:49.840 align:middle line:90%


00:35:49.840 --> 00:35:52.360 align:middle line:84%
Because, I mean, that is the
assumption that we're making--

00:35:52.360 --> 00:35:56.930 align:middle line:84%
the best responding to some
equilibrium distribution

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:59.240 align:middle line:84%
of bids, and we observe
what that distribution

00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:02.370 align:middle line:90%
is in the data.

00:36:02.370 --> 00:36:09.080 align:middle line:84%
And so this allows us to
write this down in this way.

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:12.260 align:middle line:84%
And we get-- this is now really
simple because we just take

00:36:12.260 --> 00:36:14.960 align:middle line:84%
first order conditions and
we directly arrive at this

00:36:14.960 --> 00:36:19.830 align:middle line:84%
expression here after
cancelling out and rearranging.

00:36:19.830 --> 00:36:23.310 align:middle line:84%
So this is an idea
that's also used

00:36:23.310 --> 00:36:26.340 align:middle line:84%
in repeated games and other
settings where typically

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:28.620 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium computation is hard.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:32.310 align:middle line:84%
You say, let's not even try
to first build things up

00:36:32.310 --> 00:36:34.590 align:middle line:84%
from primitives and then
compute from primitives what

00:36:34.590 --> 00:36:36.070 align:middle line:90%
the equilibrium looks like.

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:39.210 align:middle line:84%
No, we observe the
equilibrium in the data,

00:36:39.210 --> 00:36:41.170 align:middle line:84%
so we don't actually
need to compute it,

00:36:41.170 --> 00:36:42.380 align:middle line:90%
because we already have it.

00:36:42.380 --> 00:36:52.230 align:middle line:90%


00:36:52.230 --> 00:37:00.540 align:middle line:84%
So just a brief comment on
how you actually do this.

00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:03.250 align:middle line:84%
So people don't always
follow these steps.

00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:07.650 align:middle line:84%
But as you will see
later on, in terms of--

00:37:07.650 --> 00:37:13.620 align:middle line:84%
just to remind you what we need
to do to compute these v's, bid

00:37:13.620 --> 00:37:15.900 align:middle line:84%
by bid-- we have the
bids, but we still

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:19.350 align:middle line:84%
need to compute the
CDF and the PDF.

00:37:19.350 --> 00:37:21.160 align:middle line:90%
What people typically do is--

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:22.960 align:middle line:84%
of course, the CDF
is really easy.

00:37:22.960 --> 00:37:26.580 align:middle line:84%
They use kernels
for the densities.

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:29.490 align:middle line:84%
Then there are some
practical questions

00:37:29.490 --> 00:37:31.980 align:middle line:84%
like how to choose a
bandwidth, and sometimes you

00:37:31.980 --> 00:37:35.760 align:middle line:84%
want to trim in the tails
because observations,

00:37:35.760 --> 00:37:36.400 align:middle line:90%
they are noisy.

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:38.610 align:middle line:84%
And this term, as you
see, because we're

00:37:38.610 --> 00:37:42.360 align:middle line:84%
dividing by the
densities, can easily

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:46.170 align:middle line:84%
explode if the density
becomes very, very small.

00:37:46.170 --> 00:37:48.210 align:middle line:84%
And so people
sometimes throw away

00:37:48.210 --> 00:37:51.600 align:middle line:90%
some observations in the tail.

00:37:51.600 --> 00:37:56.460 align:middle line:84%
Standard errors are typically in
practice computed via bootstrap

00:37:56.460 --> 00:38:02.980 align:middle line:84%
or functions of interest
such as the reserve price

00:38:02.980 --> 00:38:10.550 align:middle line:84%
counterfactual objects that
you get out of these estimates.

00:38:10.550 --> 00:38:14.080 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:38:14.080 --> 00:38:14.740 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:38:14.740 --> 00:38:17.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so for this
one, this specific example,

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:22.670 align:middle line:84%
we still have to go back
from observing the highest

00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:27.635 align:middle line:84%
bid to the density of the
whole bid distribution, right?

00:38:27.635 --> 00:38:28.980 align:middle line:90%
TOBIAS SALZ: You may have to.

00:38:28.980 --> 00:38:30.965 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it depends
on your setting.

00:38:30.965 --> 00:38:33.257 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We're observing
only the winning bids, right?

00:38:33.257 --> 00:38:34.048 align:middle line:90%
TOBIAS SALZ: Right.

00:38:34.048 --> 00:38:36.420 align:middle line:84%
And then you can, of course,
map from the winning bid

00:38:36.420 --> 00:38:38.720 align:middle line:84%
distribution to the
bid distribution.

00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:43.220 align:middle line:84%
And you can simulate from
that bid distribution.

00:38:43.220 --> 00:38:46.370 align:middle line:90%
And with that, you can then--

00:38:46.370 --> 00:38:46.870 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:38:46.870 --> 00:38:49.620 align:middle line:90%


00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:53.730 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let me comment
on this a little bit.

00:38:53.730 --> 00:38:56.280 align:middle line:90%
This is really, really simple.

00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:59.550 align:middle line:84%
One of my advisor,
John Asker, he once

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:02.585 align:middle line:84%
joked that his most
famous paper is probably

00:39:02.585 --> 00:39:04.210 align:middle line:84%
the one on bidding
rings that uses GPV.

00:39:04.210 --> 00:39:06.640 align:middle line:84%
And he said he could have
done that paper in Excel.

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:08.820 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's a bit
of an exaggeration,

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:12.570 align:middle line:84%
but this is something
that is so easy.

00:39:12.570 --> 00:39:14.020 align:middle line:90%
I think Excel has kernels.

00:39:14.020 --> 00:39:18.240 align:middle line:84%
You can probably compute
valuations in Excel

00:39:18.240 --> 00:39:21.300 align:middle line:90%
if you wanted to.

00:39:21.300 --> 00:39:23.933 align:middle line:84%
And if you compare
this to this process

00:39:23.933 --> 00:39:25.350 align:middle line:84%
that I walked you
through earlier,

00:39:25.350 --> 00:39:27.850 align:middle line:84%
that's, of course,
a great improvement.

00:39:27.850 --> 00:39:30.658 align:middle line:90%


00:39:30.658 --> 00:39:34.190 align:middle line:84%
It has this advantage that it
does not rely on functional form

00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:36.970 align:middle line:90%
restrictions.

00:39:36.970 --> 00:39:40.990 align:middle line:84%
And as I said, this has been a
very influential paper, many,

00:39:40.990 --> 00:39:45.670 align:middle line:84%
many different extensions
to multi-unit auctions--

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:47.920 align:middle line:90%
very similar ideas apply.

00:39:47.920 --> 00:39:51.700 align:middle line:84%
If you have dynamics, you
can still typically make

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:52.930 align:middle line:90%
a lot of progress.

00:39:52.930 --> 00:39:57.040 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to talk about
Athey, Levin, and Seira

00:39:57.040 --> 00:39:59.110 align:middle line:84%
and some really
interesting applications

00:39:59.110 --> 00:40:04.810 align:middle line:84%
of using it to quantify the
damages of bidding rings.

00:40:04.810 --> 00:40:10.300 align:middle line:84%
The maybe criticism not of this
paper but of the literature

00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:14.320 align:middle line:84%
is that, well, the
IPV assumptions

00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:16.780 align:middle line:90%
are really, really convenient.

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:20.770 align:middle line:84%
And that leads people oftentimes
to use the IPV assumption

00:40:20.770 --> 00:40:22.690 align:middle line:84%
because they know
how to make progress.

00:40:22.690 --> 00:40:27.910 align:middle line:84%
And in many settings, we
believe that maybe there

00:40:27.910 --> 00:40:31.160 align:middle line:90%
is a common value component.

00:40:31.160 --> 00:40:34.330 align:middle line:84%
Bidders do not know what
they're really bidding for.

00:40:34.330 --> 00:40:36.250 align:middle line:84%
And oftentimes, there
are resale markets

00:40:36.250 --> 00:40:40.570 align:middle line:84%
or other reasons why we
believe that a common value

00:40:40.570 --> 00:40:42.810 align:middle line:90%
problem may arise.

00:40:42.810 --> 00:40:46.800 align:middle line:90%


00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:49.440 align:middle line:84%
So that's just something
to keep in mind.

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:52.260 align:middle line:84%
Maybe one more comment
on this, actually.

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:55.560 align:middle line:84%
So you may now
wonder, well, if we

00:40:55.560 --> 00:41:00.480 align:middle line:84%
believe that common values are
an element of common values

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:05.050 align:middle line:84%
in many settings, why hasn't
the literature done more

00:41:05.050 --> 00:41:05.800 align:middle line:90%
in this direction?

00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:10.230 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out that if you
follow the same steps that I

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:12.570 align:middle line:84%
just walked you
through, we basically

00:41:12.570 --> 00:41:15.090 align:middle line:90%
have mapped one distribution--

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:17.460 align:middle line:84%
we have identified one
distribution of valuations

00:41:17.460 --> 00:41:20.500 align:middle line:84%
from one observed
distribution of bids.

00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:22.020 align:middle line:84%
That means, loosely
speaking, we're

00:41:22.020 --> 00:41:28.040 align:middle line:84%
just identified in terms of
backing out this unobserved

00:41:28.040 --> 00:41:29.660 align:middle line:90%
distribution of valuations.

00:41:29.660 --> 00:41:31.370 align:middle line:84%
In a common value
setting, there are

00:41:31.370 --> 00:41:33.428 align:middle line:90%
two things that you don't know.

00:41:33.428 --> 00:41:35.720 align:middle line:84%
There is the distribution of
underlying true valuations

00:41:35.720 --> 00:41:36.840 align:middle line:90%
for the object.

00:41:36.840 --> 00:41:38.870 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
the distribution

00:41:38.870 --> 00:41:43.460 align:middle line:84%
of signals that captures
the uncertainty that--

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:47.750 align:middle line:84%
with which bidders
are forecasting what

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:49.290 align:middle line:90%
the valuation is going to be.

00:41:49.290 --> 00:41:51.320 align:middle line:84%
And these are two
unobserved objects,

00:41:51.320 --> 00:41:54.020 align:middle line:84%
but you still only observe
one distribution of bids.

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:56.150 align:middle line:84%
So you're fundamentally
underidentified

00:41:56.150 --> 00:41:58.070 align:middle line:90%
in these settings.

00:41:58.070 --> 00:42:01.520 align:middle line:84%
And so that's why
you see many often--

00:42:01.520 --> 00:42:04.590 align:middle line:90%
even when it's kind of a--

00:42:04.590 --> 00:42:10.930 align:middle line:84%
the people sticking
to the IPV assumption.

00:42:10.930 --> 00:42:19.450 align:middle line:84%
So let me now talk about a
really interesting application

00:42:19.450 --> 00:42:22.815 align:middle line:90%
by Athey, Levin, and Seira.

00:42:22.815 --> 00:42:26.600 align:middle line:90%


00:42:26.600 --> 00:42:33.290 align:middle line:84%
The question that the paper asks
is, should we use sealed bid

00:42:33.290 --> 00:42:35.665 align:middle line:90%
or open auction format?

00:42:35.665 --> 00:42:38.240 align:middle line:90%


00:42:38.240 --> 00:42:44.000 align:middle line:84%
So just to remind you,
in some instances,

00:42:44.000 --> 00:42:46.040 align:middle line:84%
the theory tells us
it shouldn't matter.

00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:48.830 align:middle line:84%
Because of revenue
equivalence, the seller

00:42:48.830 --> 00:42:52.860 align:middle line:90%
should be equally well off.

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:56.550 align:middle line:84%
But, again, there
are many things

00:42:56.550 --> 00:42:58.710 align:middle line:84%
that we believe are
very plausible to occur

00:42:58.710 --> 00:43:02.550 align:middle line:84%
in the real world, such as
bidder asymmetries, collusion,

00:43:02.550 --> 00:43:08.740 align:middle line:84%
risk aversion, and so on,
that may break this result.

00:43:08.740 --> 00:43:11.630 align:middle line:84%
So this is what they're
basically asking.

00:43:11.630 --> 00:43:15.790 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we actually test this
in the real world, what do

00:43:15.790 --> 00:43:16.690 align:middle line:90%
we find?

00:43:16.690 --> 00:43:18.880 align:middle line:84%
And they look at
this in a setting

00:43:18.880 --> 00:43:23.740 align:middle line:84%
that-- you see a lot
of timber applications

00:43:23.740 --> 00:43:27.940 align:middle line:84%
in the early auction literature
that there were some great data

00:43:27.940 --> 00:43:30.140 align:middle line:90%
available from the government.

00:43:30.140 --> 00:43:32.463 align:middle line:84%
This turned out to be a
fairly large industry.

00:43:32.463 --> 00:43:34.880 align:middle line:84%
If you're accounting for both
the logging and the milling,

00:43:34.880 --> 00:43:37.720 align:middle line:84%
it's about $100
billion industry--

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:40.510 align:middle line:90%
$100 billion a year industry.

00:43:40.510 --> 00:43:43.720 align:middle line:84%
And again, there's this
tight link between policy

00:43:43.720 --> 00:43:48.730 align:middle line:84%
and the setting and
the game that we

00:43:48.730 --> 00:43:52.960 align:middle line:84%
are estimating because the
government typically puts up

00:43:52.960 --> 00:43:57.700 align:middle line:90%
tracts of land up for auction.

00:43:57.700 --> 00:44:01.570 align:middle line:84%
And then loggers or mills
are bidding on these tracts.

00:44:01.570 --> 00:44:05.260 align:middle line:84%
So the way this works is
that they inspect or cruise

00:44:05.260 --> 00:44:07.550 align:middle line:84%
this tract and look
at what is there.

00:44:07.550 --> 00:44:12.220 align:middle line:84%
And then tracts of
lands are auctioned off.

00:44:12.220 --> 00:44:15.460 align:middle line:84%
And they have data
from what they

00:44:15.460 --> 00:44:18.130 align:middle line:84%
call the northern regions,
the Idaho-Montana border

00:44:18.130 --> 00:44:19.930 align:middle line:90%
and California.

00:44:19.930 --> 00:44:22.870 align:middle line:84%
What is really, really
nice about this setting

00:44:22.870 --> 00:44:26.110 align:middle line:84%
is that the US
Forest Service uses

00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:28.660 align:middle line:90%
both open and sealed bidding.

00:44:28.660 --> 00:44:33.090 align:middle line:84%
And in some instances, even
randomly so, it appears.

00:44:33.090 --> 00:44:37.390 align:middle line:90%


00:44:37.390 --> 00:44:40.420 align:middle line:84%
The other thing that
makes this interesting

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:42.370 align:middle line:84%
but also complicates
things is that there

00:44:42.370 --> 00:44:49.300 align:middle line:84%
has been a widespread suspicion,
believed by not just theorists

00:44:49.300 --> 00:44:52.210 align:middle line:84%
but by practitioners
in this industry,

00:44:52.210 --> 00:44:57.545 align:middle line:84%
that open auctions
foster collusion.

00:44:57.545 --> 00:44:59.170 align:middle line:84%
Actually, Congress
at some point passed

00:44:59.170 --> 00:45:02.890 align:middle line:84%
a law that says that all
these tracts should be

00:45:02.890 --> 00:45:05.150 align:middle line:84%
should be auctioned
off by sealed bidding.

00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:08.260 align:middle line:84%
And then, later on, they
started making exceptions.

00:45:08.260 --> 00:45:10.870 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that the
large bidders in this industry

00:45:10.870 --> 00:45:13.430 align:middle line:84%
really favored the
open auction format.

00:45:13.430 --> 00:45:14.980 align:middle line:90%
And you can guess why.

00:45:14.980 --> 00:45:17.960 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's a bit easier to
monitor who is doing what.

00:45:17.960 --> 00:45:20.710 align:middle line:84%
And that makes it easier
for bidders to collude.

00:45:20.710 --> 00:45:25.450 align:middle line:84%
So collusion is something that
has to be kind of accounted

00:45:25.450 --> 00:45:25.965 align:middle line:90%
for here.

00:45:25.965 --> 00:45:28.910 align:middle line:90%


00:45:28.910 --> 00:45:32.540 align:middle line:84%
So what they do
is they write down

00:45:32.540 --> 00:45:36.830 align:middle line:84%
a model that captures
some salient features

00:45:36.830 --> 00:45:38.570 align:middle line:90%
of this industry.

00:45:38.570 --> 00:45:41.360 align:middle line:84%
One salient feature
that I told you about

00:45:41.360 --> 00:45:44.370 align:middle line:84%
is that the government
uses both auction formats.

00:45:44.370 --> 00:45:47.930 align:middle line:84%
So they actually have to think
about equilibrium behavior,

00:45:47.930 --> 00:45:51.440 align:middle line:84%
both in open and
sealed bid auctions.

00:45:51.440 --> 00:45:52.940 align:middle line:84%
The other really
salient features

00:45:52.940 --> 00:45:57.040 align:middle line:84%
is that they're
loggers and mills.

00:45:57.040 --> 00:46:00.650 align:middle line:84%
Mills are basically bidders
that have some production

00:46:00.650 --> 00:46:06.380 align:middle line:84%
capacity of their own, so
they can refine and work

00:46:06.380 --> 00:46:07.790 align:middle line:90%
with the timber.

00:46:07.790 --> 00:46:10.100 align:middle line:84%
Whereas loggers, they
actually first have

00:46:10.100 --> 00:46:12.690 align:middle line:84%
to find a buyer in
the form of a mill.

00:46:12.690 --> 00:46:16.160 align:middle line:90%
So they have to pay--

00:46:16.160 --> 00:46:19.220 align:middle line:84%
they have to take an
extra cut because they

00:46:19.220 --> 00:46:21.450 align:middle line:90%
have to sell it to mills first.

00:46:21.450 --> 00:46:24.770 align:middle line:84%
And so what they're
assuming is that loggers are

00:46:24.770 --> 00:46:27.300 align:middle line:90%
weaker bidders in this setting.

00:46:27.300 --> 00:46:30.090 align:middle line:84%
And precisely, they assume
that the hazard rate

00:46:30.090 --> 00:46:32.880 align:middle line:84%
of the value of a
mill's valuations

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:34.355 align:middle line:90%
dominates the ones of loggers.

00:46:34.355 --> 00:46:39.005 align:middle line:90%


00:46:39.005 --> 00:46:41.383 align:middle line:84%
This is a setting where
there is variation

00:46:41.383 --> 00:46:42.550 align:middle line:90%
how many people are bidding.

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:46.950 align:middle line:84%
So they're accounting
for endogenous entry.

00:46:46.950 --> 00:46:51.390 align:middle line:84%
And they're searching for an
equilibrium in type symmetric

00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:52.810 align:middle line:90%
entry and bidding strategies.

00:46:52.810 --> 00:46:57.000 align:middle line:90%
So loggers and mills--

00:46:57.000 --> 00:46:59.010 align:middle line:84%
so conditioning on
loggers, they all

00:46:59.010 --> 00:47:01.200 align:middle line:84%
use the same entry
probability and bid

00:47:01.200 --> 00:47:03.480 align:middle line:84%
once they're entering
according to the same bidding

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:06.225 align:middle line:90%
function, and same for Mills.

00:47:06.225 --> 00:47:08.920 align:middle line:90%


00:47:08.920 --> 00:47:12.340 align:middle line:84%
Two more assumptions that
they're making is, first of all,

00:47:12.340 --> 00:47:14.310 align:middle line:90%
mills are not just stronger.

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:16.150 align:middle line:84%
They're basically
sufficiently strong

00:47:16.150 --> 00:47:19.990 align:middle line:84%
so that your mills are
always entering first.

00:47:19.990 --> 00:47:24.160 align:middle line:84%
So basically, the
profits of mills

00:47:24.160 --> 00:47:28.540 align:middle line:84%
with an extra mill
entering are still larger

00:47:28.540 --> 00:47:30.760 align:middle line:84%
than the profits
of loggers if there

00:47:30.760 --> 00:47:32.950 align:middle line:90%
is no such additional entrant.

00:47:32.950 --> 00:47:36.970 align:middle line:84%
And they make this
assumption to reduce

00:47:36.970 --> 00:47:38.440 align:middle line:90%
the number of equilibrium.

00:47:38.440 --> 00:47:43.570 align:middle line:84%
And the second assumption is
that only mills are colluding.

00:47:43.570 --> 00:47:48.130 align:middle line:84%
So to the extent that there
may be collusive equilibria,

00:47:48.130 --> 00:47:50.970 align:middle line:84%
they're only coming from mills
colluding with each other,

00:47:50.970 --> 00:47:54.520 align:middle line:84%
maybe because these are players
that have been in the market

00:47:54.520 --> 00:47:55.030 align:middle line:90%
for longer.

00:47:55.030 --> 00:47:58.840 align:middle line:84%
And for them, it's easier to
meet repeatedly and organize

00:47:58.840 --> 00:48:01.320 align:middle line:90%
this collusion.

00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:09.360 align:middle line:84%
So they get a number of
predictions out of the model.

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:11.460 align:middle line:84%
So the first one
is that they get

00:48:11.460 --> 00:48:15.210 align:middle line:84%
a unique type symmetric
entry equilibrium, where

00:48:15.210 --> 00:48:20.400 align:middle line:84%
either no loggers enter
or all mills enter.

00:48:20.400 --> 00:48:27.600 align:middle line:84%
So in other words, what we
cannot have is that we see

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:30.898 align:middle line:84%
a logger entering, but not
all of the mills enter.

00:48:30.898 --> 00:48:32.440 align:middle line:84%
And that's because
of this assumption

00:48:32.440 --> 00:48:38.230 align:middle line:84%
that profits are
sufficiently higher

00:48:38.230 --> 00:48:41.550 align:middle line:84%
to overcome an additional
bidder in the auction.

00:48:41.550 --> 00:48:45.320 align:middle line:90%


00:48:45.320 --> 00:48:50.090 align:middle line:84%
So then the key question
of interest, to remind you,

00:48:50.090 --> 00:48:53.090 align:middle line:84%
is, should the government
be using opened or sealed

00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:56.430 align:middle line:90%
bidding in these auctions?

00:48:56.430 --> 00:49:00.370 align:middle line:84%
And here are some comparisons
of these two auction formats.

00:49:00.370 --> 00:49:03.930 align:middle line:84%
The model tells us
that in open auctions

00:49:03.930 --> 00:49:05.550 align:middle line:84%
relative to sealed
auctions, loggers

00:49:05.550 --> 00:49:11.630 align:middle line:84%
are less likely to enter,
mills are more likely to enter,

00:49:11.630 --> 00:49:16.810 align:middle line:84%
and it is less likely
that a logger will win.

00:49:16.810 --> 00:49:21.530 align:middle line:90%


00:49:21.530 --> 00:49:24.590 align:middle line:84%
So just to explain
this a little bit,

00:49:24.590 --> 00:49:29.240 align:middle line:84%
this goes back to result in
the auction literature that's

00:49:29.240 --> 00:49:32.510 align:middle line:84%
well known by Maskin
and Riley, which

00:49:32.510 --> 00:49:36.410 align:middle line:84%
is that if you have
asymmetric bidders,

00:49:36.410 --> 00:49:40.040 align:middle line:84%
the weaker bidders tend
to bid more aggressively.

00:49:40.040 --> 00:49:42.140 align:middle line:84%
So in this case,
they would be bidding

00:49:42.140 --> 00:49:45.200 align:middle line:84%
higher markups over
their valuations

00:49:45.200 --> 00:49:48.730 align:middle line:90%
than stronger bidders.

00:49:48.730 --> 00:49:51.638 align:middle line:84%
And so this means that
sometimes-- first of all,

00:49:51.638 --> 00:49:53.930 align:middle line:84%
it means that sometimes the
auction can be inefficient.

00:49:53.930 --> 00:49:56.153 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't actually
award the object

00:49:56.153 --> 00:49:57.820 align:middle line:84%
to the bidder with
the highest valuation

00:49:57.820 --> 00:50:00.945 align:middle line:84%
because the weaker bidders
are bidding a higher markup.

00:50:00.945 --> 00:50:04.290 align:middle line:90%


00:50:04.290 --> 00:50:07.920 align:middle line:84%
And it also means that
the sealed auction format

00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:11.610 align:middle line:84%
is more attractive
to weaker bidders

00:50:11.610 --> 00:50:13.350 align:middle line:84%
because in the open
auction format,

00:50:13.350 --> 00:50:16.290 align:middle line:84%
everybody just sticks in
up to their valuation.

00:50:16.290 --> 00:50:18.720 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to be efficient,
and, more likely than not,

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:20.490 align:middle line:84%
because of the assumption
that mills are stronger,

00:50:20.490 --> 00:50:21.500 align:middle line:90%
a mill is going to win.

00:50:21.500 --> 00:50:25.294 align:middle line:90%


00:50:25.294 --> 00:50:28.150 align:middle line:84%
So that's basically--
the explanation

00:50:28.150 --> 00:50:30.580 align:middle line:84%
for these comparative
static results

00:50:30.580 --> 00:50:33.990 align:middle line:84%
is this result by
Maskin and Riley.

00:50:33.990 --> 00:50:39.010 align:middle line:90%


00:50:39.010 --> 00:50:43.690 align:middle line:84%
Lastly, they get some
other competitive static

00:50:43.690 --> 00:50:45.980 align:middle line:84%
by comparing in the
open auction format.

00:50:45.980 --> 00:50:48.760 align:middle line:84%
So they only consider
collusion in the open auction.

00:50:48.760 --> 00:50:54.160 align:middle line:84%
In the open auction
format, comparing it to--

00:50:54.160 --> 00:50:58.290 align:middle line:84%
comparing collusive and
noncollusive equilibria,

00:50:58.290 --> 00:51:01.600 align:middle line:84%
and so here it's not too
surprising that if only mills

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:05.320 align:middle line:84%
are colluding-- and remember,
they're all antisymmetric--

00:51:05.320 --> 00:51:07.000 align:middle line:84%
so with some
probability, each mill

00:51:07.000 --> 00:51:11.890 align:middle line:84%
is going to benefit from
collusion and being designated--

00:51:11.890 --> 00:51:14.200 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, one thing I forgot
to mention, I should say,

00:51:14.200 --> 00:51:16.250 align:middle line:84%
is what they assume
is perfect collusion.

00:51:16.250 --> 00:51:19.420 align:middle line:84%
So the mill with the
highest valuation

00:51:19.420 --> 00:51:23.200 align:middle line:84%
is going to be
designated the winner.

00:51:23.200 --> 00:51:25.570 align:middle line:84%
And all the other
mills are not bidding

00:51:25.570 --> 00:51:29.460 align:middle line:84%
to keep the price for
the winning mill low.

00:51:29.460 --> 00:51:33.190 align:middle line:90%


00:51:33.190 --> 00:51:36.210 align:middle line:84%
So because mills
are antisymmetric,

00:51:36.210 --> 00:51:38.800 align:middle line:84%
there's always some chance
benefit from collusion.

00:51:38.800 --> 00:51:42.130 align:middle line:84%
That means that collusive
equilibrium make this even more

00:51:42.130 --> 00:51:43.250 align:middle line:90%
attractive for mills.

00:51:43.250 --> 00:51:45.000 align:middle line:84%
So mills are going to
be more likely enter

00:51:45.000 --> 00:51:48.750 align:middle line:90%
and crowding out loggers.

00:51:48.750 --> 00:51:54.740 align:middle line:90%


00:51:54.740 --> 00:52:01.350 align:middle line:84%
So now we have some
predictions from the model.

00:52:01.350 --> 00:52:05.010 align:middle line:84%
So again, to remind you what's
really nice in this setting

00:52:05.010 --> 00:52:09.330 align:middle line:84%
is that the government,
sometimes seemingly random,

00:52:09.330 --> 00:52:14.040 align:middle line:84%
designates tracts either for
sealed or for open auction

00:52:14.040 --> 00:52:14.830 align:middle line:90%
bidding.

00:52:14.830 --> 00:52:17.100 align:middle line:84%
And they run this
regression here

00:52:17.100 --> 00:52:20.280 align:middle line:84%
where they're regressing
outcomes of interest

00:52:20.280 --> 00:52:24.540 align:middle line:84%
on a sealed indicator
plus a bunch of controls,

00:52:24.540 --> 00:52:27.450 align:middle line:84%
including in some regressions
the number of bidders

00:52:27.450 --> 00:52:29.000 align:middle line:90%
that are in the auction.

00:52:29.000 --> 00:52:31.810 align:middle line:90%


00:52:31.810 --> 00:52:35.050 align:middle line:84%
And they do this separately
by the northern regions

00:52:35.050 --> 00:52:38.890 align:middle line:90%
and in California.

00:52:38.890 --> 00:52:42.250 align:middle line:84%
The reason that this matters is
that the northern regions are--

00:52:42.250 --> 00:52:45.030 align:middle line:84%
there's more suspicion that
there's collusive behavior here.

00:52:45.030 --> 00:52:48.530 align:middle line:90%


00:52:48.530 --> 00:52:52.340 align:middle line:84%
And the other reason is that
those are actually-- they

00:52:52.340 --> 00:52:54.720 align:middle line:84%
claim that those are more
likely to be randomly assigned.

00:52:54.720 --> 00:52:57.140 align:middle line:90%
But that's just an aside.

00:52:57.140 --> 00:53:02.720 align:middle line:84%
So what they find is consistent
with what the model predicts

00:53:02.720 --> 00:53:05.330 align:middle line:84%
is that in the sealed
bidding format,

00:53:05.330 --> 00:53:08.100 align:middle line:90%
more loggers are entering.

00:53:08.100 --> 00:53:14.010 align:middle line:84%
There is no significant
effect on mill entry.

00:53:14.010 --> 00:53:19.050 align:middle line:84%
And so the fraction of
loggers in the auction

00:53:19.050 --> 00:53:22.930 align:middle line:84%
goes up in the sealed
bidding format.

00:53:22.930 --> 00:53:28.400 align:middle line:84%
Loggers are more likely
to win, but this is not--

00:53:28.400 --> 00:53:31.920 align:middle line:84%
these effects are
not significant.

00:53:31.920 --> 00:53:36.180 align:middle line:84%
And the prices in
the northern regions

00:53:36.180 --> 00:53:40.290 align:middle line:84%
are significantly
higher, both when

00:53:40.290 --> 00:53:43.470 align:middle line:84%
accounting for the number
of bidders and even-- sorry,

00:53:43.470 --> 00:53:45.690 align:middle line:84%
without accounting for
the number of bidders,

00:53:45.690 --> 00:53:47.110 align:middle line:90%
you get more entrants.

00:53:47.110 --> 00:53:51.090 align:middle line:84%
We know that more entrants
are good for sellers.

00:53:51.090 --> 00:53:53.400 align:middle line:84%
But even conditioning on
the number of bidders,

00:53:53.400 --> 00:53:55.320 align:middle line:84%
they find that
the sealed bidding

00:53:55.320 --> 00:53:56.570 align:middle line:90%
format leads to higher prices.

00:53:56.570 --> 00:54:02.170 align:middle line:90%


00:54:02.170 --> 00:54:05.380 align:middle line:84%
Largely similar
predictions for California,

00:54:05.380 --> 00:54:09.160 align:middle line:84%
with the exception that
there is no effect on prices

00:54:09.160 --> 00:54:10.770 align:middle line:84%
here from the sealed
bidding format.

00:54:10.770 --> 00:54:15.280 align:middle line:90%


00:54:15.280 --> 00:54:19.150 align:middle line:84%
OK, so in other words,
they find support

00:54:19.150 --> 00:54:22.660 align:middle line:84%
for the predictions
of the model,

00:54:22.660 --> 00:54:26.410 align:middle line:84%
that the sealed bidding
favors weaker bidders, leads

00:54:26.410 --> 00:54:29.890 align:middle line:84%
to more entry of those bidders,
and at least, in some settings,

00:54:29.890 --> 00:54:35.400 align:middle line:84%
the sealed bidding format
seems to benefit the seller.

00:54:35.400 --> 00:54:39.210 align:middle line:84%
The second takeaway
is that maybe there's

00:54:39.210 --> 00:54:43.920 align:middle line:84%
something strange going
on in the northern sales

00:54:43.920 --> 00:54:48.360 align:middle line:84%
because the sealed bidding
format leads to higher prices,

00:54:48.360 --> 00:54:50.975 align:middle line:84%
perhaps because it shuts
down this collusion channel.

00:54:50.975 --> 00:54:55.876 align:middle line:90%


00:54:55.876 --> 00:54:59.350 align:middle line:84%
So with this in
mind, what they do is

00:54:59.350 --> 00:55:02.080 align:middle line:84%
they now want to
quantify the model.

00:55:02.080 --> 00:55:05.260 align:middle line:84%
And they follow
the steps of GPV.

00:55:05.260 --> 00:55:06.980 align:middle line:84%
I'll not go through
the details here.

00:55:06.980 --> 00:55:08.985 align:middle line:84%
So they need to do this
for asymmetric bidders.

00:55:08.985 --> 00:55:11.920 align:middle line:90%


00:55:11.920 --> 00:55:14.950 align:middle line:84%
And they deviate
slightly from what

00:55:14.950 --> 00:55:17.590 align:middle line:84%
I told you people would
normally recommend that you

00:55:17.590 --> 00:55:19.390 align:middle line:90%
do to estimate a kernel.

00:55:19.390 --> 00:55:24.100 align:middle line:84%
They actually estimate a
parametric function for bids.

00:55:24.100 --> 00:55:26.320 align:middle line:90%
But these are just details.

00:55:26.320 --> 00:55:30.370 align:middle line:84%
One thing that they do
that I do want to mention--

00:55:30.370 --> 00:55:32.620 align:middle line:84%
although I'm not talking
about the details here;

00:55:32.620 --> 00:55:34.180 align:middle line:84%
we're going to do
this in [14.]273--

00:55:34.180 --> 00:55:39.650 align:middle line:84%
is you could be in many settings
worried about common values.

00:55:39.650 --> 00:55:41.650 align:middle line:84%
But yet another thing you
could be worried about

00:55:41.650 --> 00:55:45.280 align:middle line:84%
is that there's something that
all the bidders are actually

00:55:45.280 --> 00:55:46.010 align:middle line:90%
observing.

00:55:46.010 --> 00:55:48.380 align:middle line:84%
So they do not have any
uncertainty about this

00:55:48.380 --> 00:55:52.190 align:middle line:84%
but the econometrician
does not observe.

00:55:52.190 --> 00:55:56.630 align:middle line:84%
So maybe there's some tracts
here in the setting where,

00:55:56.630 --> 00:55:59.630 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, there's a steep
incline or something that

00:55:59.630 --> 00:56:06.890 align:middle line:84%
makes it much more
difficult to log the trees.

00:56:06.890 --> 00:56:09.500 align:middle line:84%
The bidders all know this,
but this might not necessarily

00:56:09.500 --> 00:56:12.730 align:middle line:90%
be recorded in our data.

00:56:12.730 --> 00:56:15.730 align:middle line:84%
So that means that you find
that the bids from one auction

00:56:15.730 --> 00:56:19.370 align:middle line:90%
to another are correlated.

00:56:19.370 --> 00:56:21.590 align:middle line:84%
And if you do not
account for the fact

00:56:21.590 --> 00:56:24.590 align:middle line:84%
that the bidders all observed
this and take this into account,

00:56:24.590 --> 00:56:28.550 align:middle line:84%
you might be overstating
the allocative efficiency

00:56:28.550 --> 00:56:30.980 align:middle line:84%
of auctions because you
load all this heterogeneity

00:56:30.980 --> 00:56:33.823 align:middle line:90%
as cross-bidder heterogeneity.

00:56:33.823 --> 00:56:35.240 align:middle line:84%
You're not accounting
for the fact

00:56:35.240 --> 00:56:37.130 align:middle line:84%
that some auctions
look really good,

00:56:37.130 --> 00:56:39.800 align:middle line:84%
some auctions are really
bad, and the average prices

00:56:39.800 --> 00:56:42.890 align:middle line:84%
in each auction are
accounting for that.

00:56:42.890 --> 00:56:44.326 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:56:44.326 --> 00:56:47.450 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What are the timing
of the auctions for the tracts?

00:56:47.450 --> 00:56:49.890 align:middle line:84%
Are they occurring
simultaneously?

00:56:49.890 --> 00:56:51.920 align:middle line:84%
Is there kind of like
a continuous time

00:56:51.920 --> 00:56:54.500 align:middle line:84%
aspect that the tracts to
open up in the auction?

00:56:54.500 --> 00:56:56.750 align:middle line:84%
So I guess the question
is, how much does it

00:56:56.750 --> 00:57:01.852 align:middle line:84%
become a multi-unit
auction versus not?

00:57:01.852 --> 00:57:04.798 align:middle line:84%
TOBIAS SALZ: I mean, so
I don't know exactly how

00:57:04.798 --> 00:57:06.090 align:middle line:90%
the timing works, to be honest.

00:57:06.090 --> 00:57:09.380 align:middle line:90%
But there are many auctions.

00:57:09.380 --> 00:57:14.780 align:middle line:84%
And this goes-- there are
potential dynamics that

00:57:14.780 --> 00:57:15.720 align:middle line:90%
result from that.

00:57:15.720 --> 00:57:19.610 align:middle line:84%
So I think Susan and others
they have or maybe Pat Bajari,

00:57:19.610 --> 00:57:23.840 align:middle line:84%
they have a paper on capacity
constraints of bidders

00:57:23.840 --> 00:57:26.280 align:middle line:84%
or other dynamic
things that may matter.

00:57:26.280 --> 00:57:29.210 align:middle line:84%
So they occur frequently enough
that these kinds of things

00:57:29.210 --> 00:57:31.405 align:middle line:90%
might potentially be relevant.

00:57:31.405 --> 00:57:31.905 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:57:31.905 --> 00:57:35.470 align:middle line:90%


00:57:35.470 --> 00:57:37.130 align:middle line:84%
I hope that answers
your question.

00:57:37.130 --> 00:57:41.170 align:middle line:90%


00:57:41.170 --> 00:57:42.170 align:middle line:90%
Where was I?

00:57:42.170 --> 00:57:45.310 align:middle line:84%
OK, I was talking about this
unobserved auction-specific

00:57:45.310 --> 00:57:46.460 align:middle line:90%
heterogeneity.

00:57:46.460 --> 00:57:51.190 align:middle line:84%
Again, I might see that there's
a tract with low-quality wood

00:57:51.190 --> 00:57:54.740 align:middle line:84%
and every bidder
appropriately prices this in.

00:57:54.740 --> 00:57:58.870 align:middle line:84%
And so there's basically
a way to account

00:57:58.870 --> 00:58:02.920 align:middle line:84%
for this that comes out of the
nonlinear measurement error

00:58:02.920 --> 00:58:03.860 align:middle line:90%
literature.

00:58:03.860 --> 00:58:08.050 align:middle line:84%
The key paper here is
the one by Li and Vuong.

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:11.770 align:middle line:84%
And this has been extended
to asymmetric auctions

00:58:11.770 --> 00:58:13.450 align:middle line:90%
by Krasnokutskaya.

00:58:13.450 --> 00:58:16.240 align:middle line:84%
So this basically allows
to account for the fact

00:58:16.240 --> 00:58:18.730 align:middle line:84%
that there are systematic
differences across auctions

00:58:18.730 --> 00:58:21.830 align:middle line:90%
that bidders do not account for.

00:58:21.830 --> 00:58:24.190 align:middle line:84%
And that, again, matters
because otherwise you

00:58:24.190 --> 00:58:27.440 align:middle line:84%
would say that this
heterogeneity is across bidders.

00:58:27.440 --> 00:58:32.800 align:middle line:84%
And then that would give us
oftentimes very, very wrong

00:58:32.800 --> 00:58:36.015 align:middle line:84%
estimates for, let's say,
the efficiency of an auction.

00:58:36.015 --> 00:58:40.180 align:middle line:90%


00:58:40.180 --> 00:58:42.820 align:middle line:90%
They estimate optimal entry--

00:58:42.820 --> 00:58:44.380 align:middle line:84%
and something really
neat that they

00:58:44.380 --> 00:58:46.780 align:middle line:84%
do that I want to
discuss a little bit

00:58:46.780 --> 00:58:54.520 align:middle line:84%
is they estimate only based on
sealed bidding using GPV to have

00:58:54.520 --> 00:58:55.580 align:middle line:90%
the open-auction format.

00:58:55.580 --> 00:58:57.080 align:middle line:84%
They have the sealed
bidding format.

00:58:57.080 --> 00:58:59.350 align:middle line:84%
They only estimate
based on sealed bidding,

00:58:59.350 --> 00:59:04.650 align:middle line:84%
but they generate predictions
for both auction formats.

00:59:04.650 --> 00:59:06.810 align:middle line:84%
So that is a really
strong test if you

00:59:06.810 --> 00:59:08.077 align:middle line:90%
think about it of the model.

00:59:08.077 --> 00:59:12.360 align:middle line:90%


00:59:12.360 --> 00:59:14.910 align:middle line:84%
You're estimating the valuations
based on sealed bidding,

00:59:14.910 --> 00:59:19.930 align:middle line:84%
and you generate predictions
for the open auction.

00:59:19.930 --> 00:59:25.100 align:middle line:84%
So let's see kind of
how well they're doing.

00:59:25.100 --> 00:59:32.570 align:middle line:84%
So here is-- here's a table
that compares separately

00:59:32.570 --> 00:59:34.940 align:middle line:84%
for the northern
sales in California,

00:59:34.940 --> 00:59:39.300 align:middle line:84%
the out-of-sample
performance of their models.

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:43.300 align:middle line:84%
So they have some
holdout auctions.

00:59:43.300 --> 00:59:47.060 align:middle line:84%
And they simulate outcomes
for this holdout auction

00:59:47.060 --> 00:59:49.550 align:middle line:84%
from their model and
compare it to what actually

00:59:49.550 --> 00:59:50.970 align:middle line:90%
happened in those auctions.

00:59:50.970 --> 00:59:53.870 align:middle line:84%
And then they do this
for a sealed bid.

00:59:53.870 --> 00:59:57.110 align:middle line:84%
So holding the mechanism on
which they estimate constant,

00:59:57.110 --> 00:59:59.450 align:middle line:84%
and they also do this
for the open auction

00:59:59.450 --> 01:00:03.240 align:middle line:84%
format, which they actually
never use for estimation.

01:00:03.240 --> 01:00:05.950 align:middle line:90%


01:00:05.950 --> 01:00:10.630 align:middle line:84%
So what they find here is
you can go through this--

01:00:10.630 --> 01:00:12.770 align:middle line:84%
so the big picture
is for sealed bids,

01:00:12.770 --> 01:00:14.440 align:middle line:84%
the model does
really, really well,

01:00:14.440 --> 01:00:16.900 align:middle line:90%
as we would typically expect.

01:00:16.900 --> 01:00:18.710 align:middle line:90%
If you think about--

01:00:18.710 --> 01:00:22.420 align:middle line:84%
if you think back to
what we did in GPV,

01:00:22.420 --> 01:00:27.170 align:middle line:84%
we rationalized
each bid exactly,

01:00:27.170 --> 01:00:29.450 align:middle line:84%
by finding a valuation that
matches each bid exactly.

01:00:29.450 --> 01:00:32.780 align:middle line:84%
So you would actually think
that if you follow exactly

01:00:32.780 --> 01:00:35.210 align:middle line:84%
all the steps, then your
model should predict

01:00:35.210 --> 01:00:37.910 align:middle line:90%
exactly what is happening.

01:00:37.910 --> 01:00:41.340 align:middle line:84%
Here, there's some things that
deviate a little bit from this.

01:00:41.340 --> 01:00:44.180 align:middle line:84%
They put a parametric
distribution on the bid,

01:00:44.180 --> 01:00:46.070 align:middle line:90%
so that might--

01:00:46.070 --> 01:00:54.140 align:middle line:84%
that could introduce
some prediction error.

01:00:54.140 --> 01:01:00.410 align:middle line:84%
But what they basically find
is that the average bid is very

01:01:00.410 --> 01:01:04.110 align:middle line:90%
close to what is predicted.

01:01:04.110 --> 01:01:08.910 align:middle line:84%
And that's true whether or
not you account for entry.

01:01:08.910 --> 01:01:13.530 align:middle line:84%
Same for the average revenue is
very close to what is predicted.

01:01:13.530 --> 01:01:19.010 align:middle line:84%
And your average logger
entry is almost exactly--

01:01:19.010 --> 01:01:22.910 align:middle line:90%
I mean, is exactly spot on.

01:01:22.910 --> 01:01:25.250 align:middle line:84%
And just very
briefly, if you look

01:01:25.250 --> 01:01:28.460 align:middle line:84%
at sealed bids for
California, the same is true.

01:01:28.460 --> 01:01:31.800 align:middle line:84%
So logger entry is
predicted perfectly.

01:01:31.800 --> 01:01:37.340 align:middle line:84%
You get very close
values for average bids.

01:01:37.340 --> 01:01:39.650 align:middle line:84%
And I guess average
revenue in California

01:01:39.650 --> 01:01:41.730 align:middle line:90%
is somewhat overpredicted.

01:01:41.730 --> 01:01:46.010 align:middle line:90%


01:01:46.010 --> 01:01:51.170 align:middle line:84%
So let me actually stick
to this slide for a second.

01:01:51.170 --> 01:01:57.780 align:middle line:84%
For the open auction format,
and I find this amazing,

01:01:57.780 --> 01:02:04.430 align:middle line:84%
they also predict,
at least for the case

01:02:04.430 --> 01:02:07.850 align:middle line:84%
of California, the average
sales price and entry very,

01:02:07.850 --> 01:02:10.910 align:middle line:90%
very closely.

01:02:10.910 --> 01:02:13.030 align:middle line:90%
So again, this is a pretty--

01:02:13.030 --> 01:02:15.560 align:middle line:84%
I haven't seen this
done in many papers.

01:02:15.560 --> 01:02:17.645 align:middle line:84%
This is a pretty strong
test of the model,

01:02:17.645 --> 01:02:19.270 align:middle line:84%
that you take it to
a different setting

01:02:19.270 --> 01:02:23.040 align:middle line:84%
and then see how well
it performs there.

01:02:23.040 --> 01:02:26.102 align:middle line:90%


01:02:26.102 --> 01:02:30.380 align:middle line:84%
What they then do is because
there was suspicion of collusion

01:02:30.380 --> 01:02:34.700 align:middle line:84%
in the northern sales,
they also generate

01:02:34.700 --> 01:02:38.060 align:middle line:84%
for the open auction
format predictions

01:02:38.060 --> 01:02:41.000 align:middle line:90%
from a collusive model--

01:02:41.000 --> 01:02:43.370 align:middle line:84%
the way they generate
a collusive model is

01:02:43.370 --> 01:02:47.877 align:middle line:84%
to take a simulation,
draw the highest valuation

01:02:47.877 --> 01:02:49.710 align:middle line:84%
bidder is going to be
designated the winner.

01:02:49.710 --> 01:02:51.910 align:middle line:90%
The other mills are not--

01:02:51.910 --> 01:02:55.520 align:middle line:90%
are bidding zero.

01:02:55.520 --> 01:03:00.350 align:middle line:84%
And so that leads to
much, much lower prices.

01:03:00.350 --> 01:03:02.960 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, so
much so that they,

01:03:02.960 --> 01:03:07.520 align:middle line:84%
even for the northern sales,
where the collusion was

01:03:07.520 --> 01:03:09.680 align:middle line:84%
potentially an
issue, underpredict

01:03:09.680 --> 01:03:13.070 align:middle line:84%
the average sales
price by about a third.

01:03:13.070 --> 01:03:18.110 align:middle line:84%
But they do also reject
the competitive model

01:03:18.110 --> 01:03:20.720 align:middle line:84%
for the case of
the northern sales.

01:03:20.720 --> 01:03:25.140 align:middle line:84%
So in this instance, they're
rejecting both models.

01:03:25.140 --> 01:03:28.530 align:middle line:84%
And so what they then argue
is that maybe what best

01:03:28.530 --> 01:03:31.200 align:middle line:84%
explains the data is a moderate
form of collusion, where

01:03:31.200 --> 01:03:33.600 align:middle line:84%
some of the mills are
colluding or some of the mills

01:03:33.600 --> 01:03:37.170 align:middle line:90%
are colluding some of the time.

01:03:37.170 --> 01:03:41.430 align:middle line:90%
So kind of my--

01:03:41.430 --> 01:03:44.810 align:middle line:84%
my view on this paper, this is
a very complete and well-done

01:03:44.810 --> 01:03:45.310 align:middle line:90%
paper.

01:03:45.310 --> 01:03:48.780 align:middle line:84%
In fact, it does so many
things that I didn't have time

01:03:48.780 --> 01:03:50.530 align:middle line:90%
to talk about all of them.

01:03:50.530 --> 01:03:53.760 align:middle line:84%
But it has taken a pretty
big bite out of the apple.

01:03:53.760 --> 01:03:58.230 align:middle line:84%
Many following papers do
focus on one aspect, collusion

01:03:58.230 --> 01:04:01.380 align:middle line:90%
or entry.

01:04:01.380 --> 01:04:05.220 align:middle line:84%
And here, they do many
of these things together.

01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:12.510 align:middle line:84%
And it's basically theory
testing plus a setting

01:04:12.510 --> 01:04:15.210 align:middle line:84%
where you can use
some random variation

01:04:15.210 --> 01:04:21.780 align:middle line:84%
to be pretty confident that
the model that you're selecting

01:04:21.780 --> 01:04:24.130 align:middle line:84%
is the right one, plus then
the structural exercise.

01:04:24.130 --> 01:04:26.862 align:middle line:84%
So this is pretty good
blueprint for a job market paper

01:04:26.862 --> 01:04:29.320 align:middle line:84%
because it has kind of all
these different things in there.

01:04:29.320 --> 01:04:33.770 align:middle line:90%


01:04:33.770 --> 01:04:37.550 align:middle line:84%
So maybe some things that one
could complain about, again,

01:04:37.550 --> 01:04:39.380 align:middle line:90%
is this a private value setting?

01:04:39.380 --> 01:04:44.970 align:middle line:84%
Certainly there are resale
markets for the timber.

01:04:44.970 --> 01:04:50.120 align:middle line:84%
And so that, in itself,
introduces a common value aspect

01:04:50.120 --> 01:04:56.653 align:middle line:84%
because everybody can sell
at that resale market price.

01:04:56.653 --> 01:04:58.070 align:middle line:84%
And maybe there's
some uncertainty

01:04:58.070 --> 01:05:01.460 align:middle line:84%
about the quality of the
timber or how difficult

01:05:01.460 --> 01:05:06.410 align:middle line:90%
it is to get it.

01:05:06.410 --> 01:05:08.480 align:middle line:84%
And then the other
question is, can we

01:05:08.480 --> 01:05:10.520 align:middle line:84%
attribute part of
this effect that we

01:05:10.520 --> 01:05:13.880 align:middle line:84%
see that the sealed bidding
format generates much higher

01:05:13.880 --> 01:05:17.720 align:middle line:90%
revenue to risk aversion?

01:05:17.720 --> 01:05:22.430 align:middle line:90%
And so I want to use my last--

01:05:22.430 --> 01:05:23.600 align:middle line:90%
I'm going a bit fast today.

01:05:23.600 --> 01:05:29.000 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to
use my last minutes

01:05:29.000 --> 01:05:34.220 align:middle line:84%
to talk about another
paper by Yunmi Kong, who

01:05:34.220 --> 01:05:36.980 align:middle line:84%
was my classmate in
grad school, which

01:05:36.980 --> 01:05:43.820 align:middle line:84%
thinks about risk aversion in
open and sealed bid auctions.

01:05:43.820 --> 01:05:46.410 align:middle line:84%
So the setting here is a
very different setting.

01:05:46.410 --> 01:05:50.670 align:middle line:84%
It's the Permian
Basin in New Mexico.

01:05:50.670 --> 01:05:53.660 align:middle line:90%
So the Permian Basin is a big--

01:05:53.660 --> 01:05:55.580 align:middle line:84%
one of the most
productive oil fields

01:05:55.580 --> 01:06:01.960 align:middle line:84%
in the world that spans New
Mexico and the west of Texas.

01:06:01.960 --> 01:06:07.510 align:middle line:84%
And here we have
many smaller firms,

01:06:07.510 --> 01:06:10.960 align:middle line:84%
oftentimes operated
by just a few people,

01:06:10.960 --> 01:06:18.270 align:middle line:84%
that lease land from the
government and that rent

01:06:18.270 --> 01:06:23.340 align:middle line:84%
equipment-- here you see an
oil rig in this setting--

01:06:23.340 --> 01:06:26.610 align:middle line:84%
that rent equipment,
and basically,

01:06:26.610 --> 01:06:29.640 align:middle line:84%
through these auctions from the
New Mexico State and Land Lease

01:06:29.640 --> 01:06:36.360 align:middle line:84%
Office, get the land, and try
their luck and drill for oil.

01:06:36.360 --> 01:06:40.950 align:middle line:84%
And so what they're bidding
on is a lump sum bonus.

01:06:40.950 --> 01:06:43.230 align:middle line:84%
And then for the time
that they're renting,

01:06:43.230 --> 01:06:47.560 align:middle line:84%
they have to pay royalties
and a rental rate.

01:06:47.560 --> 01:06:54.170 align:middle line:84%
What's nice is that, as in the
previous paper, it seems that--

01:06:54.170 --> 01:06:55.780 align:middle line:90%
So the government uses both.

01:06:55.780 --> 01:06:58.570 align:middle line:84%
Or the New Mexico
State Land Office

01:06:58.570 --> 01:07:00.740 align:middle line:84%
uses both open and
sealed bidding again.

01:07:00.740 --> 01:07:03.340 align:middle line:84%
So we can get a
similar comparison

01:07:03.340 --> 01:07:07.270 align:middle line:84%
as before in a very
different setting.

01:07:07.270 --> 01:07:12.700 align:middle line:84%
And if you stare at the map of
how these formats are spatially

01:07:12.700 --> 01:07:15.730 align:middle line:84%
distributed, this is
open, this is sealed,

01:07:15.730 --> 01:07:19.220 align:middle line:90%
it looks pretty random.

01:07:19.220 --> 01:07:24.020 align:middle line:84%
So again, we can use
basically with some controls

01:07:24.020 --> 01:07:26.960 align:middle line:84%
the fact that this looks
almost random to look

01:07:26.960 --> 01:07:32.410 align:middle line:84%
at the effect of
different auction formats.

01:07:32.410 --> 01:07:35.650 align:middle line:90%
So here's what she finds.

01:07:35.650 --> 01:07:38.500 align:middle line:90%


01:07:38.500 --> 01:07:42.040 align:middle line:84%
As before for the
northern sales,

01:07:42.040 --> 01:07:47.710 align:middle line:84%
sealed bidding leads with a
bunch of controls for oil prices

01:07:47.710 --> 01:07:52.600 align:middle line:84%
and differences of these tracts
of land leads to about 30%

01:07:52.600 --> 01:07:56.325 align:middle line:84%
higher revenue than the
open auction format.

01:07:56.325 --> 01:08:03.250 align:middle line:90%


01:08:03.250 --> 01:08:06.730 align:middle line:84%
The other really interesting
observation that she makes

01:08:06.730 --> 01:08:12.790 align:middle line:84%
is she compares the
distribution of prices

01:08:12.790 --> 01:08:16.000 align:middle line:90%
across open and sealed bidding.

01:08:16.000 --> 01:08:19.930 align:middle line:84%
So here are the histograms
put on top of each other.

01:08:19.930 --> 01:08:25.905 align:middle line:84%
And you see for the open auction
format this big spike here.

01:08:25.905 --> 01:08:29.054 align:middle line:90%


01:08:29.054 --> 01:08:32.260 align:middle line:84%
So the reason this happens is
that oftentimes bidder enter

01:08:32.260 --> 01:08:34.420 align:middle line:84%
the open auction format,
they're discovering

01:08:34.420 --> 01:08:37.125 align:middle line:84%
they're the only bidder, they're
bidding the reserve price.

01:08:37.125 --> 01:08:40.569 align:middle line:90%


01:08:40.569 --> 01:08:42.970 align:middle line:84%
Interestingly, although
the distribution

01:08:42.970 --> 01:08:46.540 align:middle line:84%
of the number of bidders
for the sealed bid auctions

01:08:46.540 --> 01:08:50.770 align:middle line:84%
is almost identical, and there
are many auctions that also only

01:08:50.770 --> 01:08:53.475 align:middle line:84%
have one bidder, we do
not see such a mass point.

01:08:53.475 --> 01:08:57.191 align:middle line:90%


01:08:57.191 --> 01:09:01.543 align:middle line:84%
So what this suggests is
that the bidders do not

01:09:01.543 --> 01:09:03.960 align:middle line:84%
know how many other bidders
are going to be in the auction

01:09:03.960 --> 01:09:06.327 align:middle line:84%
when they engage
in sealed bidding.

01:09:06.327 --> 01:09:08.160 align:middle line:84%
So they have uncertainty,
and they basically

01:09:08.160 --> 01:09:10.710 align:middle line:84%
want to insure themselves
against competitors

01:09:10.710 --> 01:09:14.850 align:middle line:84%
by bidding higher
than what they would

01:09:14.850 --> 01:09:18.488 align:middle line:84%
if they were the only bidder,
even though with some chance

01:09:18.488 --> 01:09:20.030 align:middle line:84%
they are going to
be the only bidder.

01:09:20.030 --> 01:09:24.120 align:middle line:90%


01:09:24.120 --> 01:09:29.310 align:middle line:84%
So this observation together
with the previous observation

01:09:29.310 --> 01:09:36.000 align:middle line:84%
that the revenue for sealed
bid auction seems to be higher

01:09:36.000 --> 01:09:43.420 align:middle line:84%
leads Yunmi to conclude that
risk aversion is probably

01:09:43.420 --> 01:09:45.029 align:middle line:90%
the explanation for this.

01:09:45.029 --> 01:09:50.819 align:middle line:84%
The reason is that
uncertainty is revenue

01:09:50.819 --> 01:09:59.580 align:middle line:84%
neutral for the seller but only
if the bidders are-- sorry,

01:09:59.580 --> 01:10:02.065 align:middle line:84%
if the bidders are
risk neutral, then

01:10:02.065 --> 01:10:03.690 align:middle line:84%
uncertainty about
the number of bidders

01:10:03.690 --> 01:10:07.800 align:middle line:84%
is going to be revenue
neutral for the seller.

01:10:07.800 --> 01:10:09.490 align:middle line:90%
And that's not what we observe.

01:10:09.490 --> 01:10:12.320 align:middle line:84%
So that's a theoretical
prediction from Harstad, Kagel,

01:10:12.320 --> 01:10:14.340 align:middle line:90%
and another Levin.

01:10:14.340 --> 01:10:16.320 align:middle line:90%
And that's not what we observe.

01:10:16.320 --> 01:10:17.820 align:middle line:84%
We see higher revenue
for the seller

01:10:17.820 --> 01:10:19.410 align:middle line:84%
from the sealed
bid auction, which

01:10:19.410 --> 01:10:23.613 align:middle line:84%
is consistent with this idea
that bidders insuring themselves

01:10:23.613 --> 01:10:25.530 align:middle line:84%
against competition in
the sealed bid auctions

01:10:25.530 --> 01:10:29.100 align:middle line:90%
by bidding higher.

01:10:29.100 --> 01:10:34.500 align:middle line:84%
So I think this is
a clever detective

01:10:34.500 --> 01:10:40.757 align:middle line:84%
work to make the case that even
when companies are competing

01:10:40.757 --> 01:10:42.840 align:middle line:84%
against each other, where
we typically assume risk

01:10:42.840 --> 01:10:45.270 align:middle line:84%
neutrality, at least
in this setting, where

01:10:45.270 --> 01:10:47.250 align:middle line:84%
many of the companies
are small, it

01:10:47.250 --> 01:10:50.250 align:middle line:84%
seems to be that risk
aversion plays a big role

01:10:50.250 --> 01:10:54.090 align:middle line:84%
and leads to higher revenue
for the seller and sealed bid

01:10:54.090 --> 01:10:55.580 align:middle line:90%
auctions.

01:10:55.580 --> 01:11:01.040 align:middle line:84%
And she then goes on
to show that the model,

01:11:01.040 --> 01:11:03.740 align:middle line:84%
a model with risk aversion,
where now you not just have

01:11:03.740 --> 01:11:05.990 align:middle line:84%
to identify the underlying
distribution of valuations,

01:11:05.990 --> 01:11:09.260 align:middle line:84%
you also need to identify
the level of risk aversion

01:11:09.260 --> 01:11:13.610 align:middle line:84%
and privacy-- like I said we
just identified in the standard

01:11:13.610 --> 01:11:14.900 align:middle line:90%
case--

01:11:14.900 --> 01:11:19.490 align:middle line:84%
so here she basically uses
both the open bidding format

01:11:19.490 --> 01:11:21.500 align:middle line:84%
and the sealed bidding
format together

01:11:21.500 --> 01:11:25.730 align:middle line:84%
to show that you can identify
the distribution of valuations

01:11:25.730 --> 01:11:30.410 align:middle line:84%
of bidders as well as the
level of risk aversion.

01:11:30.410 --> 01:11:32.520 align:middle line:84%
So it's a nice paper
that's in RAND.

01:11:32.520 --> 01:11:36.720 align:middle line:90%


01:11:36.720 --> 01:11:40.380 align:middle line:90%
OK, ending early.

01:11:40.380 --> 01:11:42.570 align:middle line:90%
That's all I have for today.

01:11:42.570 --> 01:11:48.660 align:middle line:84%
I hope I see many of you
next semester in [14.]273,

01:11:48.660 --> 01:11:53.630 align:middle line:84%
where we dive deeper
into all of these topics.

01:11:53.630 --> 01:12:06.000 align:middle line:90%